window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["lambda"]=[{"id":"lambda-f0001","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"What problem does serverless compute solve compared with running your own servers?","a":"<p>You stop managing servers. AWS handles provisioning, patching, scaling and availability, and you pay only for the work done. A small team can ship a backend without capacity planning, and idle time costs nothing. The trade-off is less control over the OS and runtime, and limits such as the 15-minute timeout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0002","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are the three steps from writing code to having it run?","a":"<p>Upload the code (zip or container image) with a runtime and handler, configure a trigger (an API call, an S3 event, a queue, a schedule), and let Lambda run the handler whenever the trigger fires. Lambda creates the execution environment, passes the event to the handler and scales environments with the number of concurrent events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0003","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"What does \"scales to zero\" mean for Lambda, and what is the catch?","a":"<p>With no events there are no running environments and no compute charge. The catch is that the first request after idle time hits a cold start, because a new environment has to be initialized.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0004","card":"q-lambda-001-serverless-compute-and-how-lambda-works","sec":"lambda-s002","q":"In AWS Lambda, what does this handler return, and who consumes that return value?","a":"<p>It returns an object with <code>statusCode</code> and a JSON-string <code>body</code>. For a synchronous caller such as API Gateway, that object becomes the HTTP response. For an asynchronous trigger such as S3 the return value is ignored. <code>console.log</code> output goes to CloudWatch Logs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n    console.log(&#x27;Event:&#x27;, JSON.stringify(event));\n    const response = {\n        statusCode: 200,\n        body: JSON.stringify(&#x27;Processed event successfully!&#x27;)\n    };\n    return response;\n};</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0005","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"Name good use cases for AWS Lambda and say why each fits.","a":"<p>Event-driven glue and short, bursty work: API backends behind API Gateway, file processing on S3 upload, stream or queue consumers, scheduled jobs, and automation reacting to AWS events. They fit because the load is spiky, each unit of work is short, and idle time should cost nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0006","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"When is Lambda a poor choice?","a":"<p>Long-running jobs (over 15 minutes), workloads needing specialized hardware such as GPUs, steady high-throughput load where an always-on instance or container is cheaper, very latency-critical paths where cold starts are unacceptable, and workloads needing persistent connections or local state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0007","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"In AWS Lambda, how would you stop unused EC2 instances every evening?","a":"<p>Create an EventBridge schedule (a <code>cron()</code> expression) that invokes a Lambda function. The function calls the EC2 <code>StopInstances</code> API. Its execution role needs <code>ec2:StopInstances</code> (plus <code>ec2:DescribeInstances</code> if it looks instances up by tag), scoped to the right resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0008","card":"q-lambda-002-lambda-use-cases-and-when-not-to-use-it","sec":"lambda-s003","q":"A job processes a large video and takes 40 minutes. What are your options on AWS instead of a single Lambda function?","a":"<p>Lambda stops at 15 minutes. Split the work into chunks and orchestrate them with Step Functions, or run the job on AWS Fargate, ECS, AWS Batch or EC2. Pick Lambda chunks only if the work is naturally divisible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0009","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"In AWS Lambda, how do you estimate the monthly cost of 1 million invocations at 200 ms each with 128 MB of memory?","a":"<p>Compute is GB-seconds: 1,000,000 x 0.2 s x 0.125 GB = 25,000 GB-seconds, which you multiply by the per-GB-second price. The request charge (per million requests) is added. The monthly free tier is subtracted first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0010","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"What costs extra on Lambda beyond requests and duration?","a":"<p>Provisioned Concurrency (pay for pre-warmed capacity), ephemeral storage beyond the default size, data transfer out, and the services around the function (API Gateway, CloudWatch Logs, NAT gateway, and so on). Logging and NAT charges often surprise people.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0011","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"When does Lambda become more expensive than EC2 or containers?","a":"<p>When load is steady and high. Per-ms pricing carries a premium over a fully utilized instance, so a function running near-constantly at high concurrency can cost more than a reserved instance or Fargate task. Lambda wins for spiky or low-utilization traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0012","card":"q-lambda-003-pricing-model","sec":"lambda-s004","q":"In AWS Lambda, how does choosing the arm64 (Graviton) architecture affect price?","a":"<p>arm64 is billed at a lower price per GB-second than x86_64 (about 20% lower) and is often as fast or faster. Native dependencies must be built for arm64, so check packages and layers first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0013","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"What is AWS Lambda, and how does serverless compute differ from EC2?","a":"<p>Lambda is a serverless, event-driven compute service that runs your code in response to events without you provisioning or managing servers. You upload code, define a trigger, and AWS handles the servers, scaling, patching and availability. With EC2 you rent and manage a server (OS, scaling, patching) and pay while it runs. With Lambda you pay only per request and per ms of execution, it scales to zero when idle, and it scales out automatically per request.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0014","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"Explain the concept of event-driven execution in Lambda.","a":"<p>Lambda runs only when an event occurs: an HTTP call via API Gateway, an object uploaded to S3, a message in SQS, a DynamoDB stream record or a scheduled EventBridge rule. Each event reaches the handler as the <code>event</code> object. No event means no execution and no cost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0015","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"What is the execution model of AWS Lambda (stateless compute)?","a":"<p>Each invocation is stateless. Do not rely on in-memory data persisting across requests, because AWS may create a fresh execution environment at any time. Keep state in external stores (DynamoDB, S3, RDS, ElastiCache). The environment can be reused for warm invocations, so anything outside the handler (SDK clients, connections) can be cached opportunistically.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># WRONG: relying on in-memory state across requests\ncounter = 0\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    global counter\n    counter += 1          # NOT reliable: new env resets this to 0\n    return counter\n\n# RIGHT: persist state externally (e.g., DynamoDB atomic counter)\nimport boto3\nddb = boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    r = ddb.update_item(\n        TableName=&quot;Counters&quot;, Key={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: &quot;hits&quot;}},\n        UpdateExpression=&quot;ADD #c :one&quot;,\n        ExpressionAttributeNames={&quot;#c&quot;: &quot;count&quot;},\n        ExpressionAttributeValues={&quot;:one&quot;: {&quot;N&quot;: &quot;1&quot;}},\n        ReturnValues=&quot;UPDATED_NEW&quot;)\n    return r[&quot;Attributes&quot;][&quot;count&quot;][&quot;N&quot;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0016","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"What is wrong with this Lambda handler, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The counter lives in one execution environment's memory. Another concurrent request may run in a different environment, and a new environment starts again at 0, so the count is wrong. Keep shared state in an external store, for example a DynamoDB atomic counter.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>counter = 0\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    global counter\n    counter += 1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0017","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"When would you pick EC2 over Lambda for a web backend?","a":"<p>Pick EC2 for steady heavy traffic, long-lived connections, a need for OS-level control, special hardware, or software that cannot fit Lambda's limits (15 minutes, memory, package size). Pick Lambda when traffic is irregular, the team wants no server management, and requests are short.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0018","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"In AWS Lambda, how many requests does one execution environment handle at the same time?","a":"<p>One. Each environment processes a single invocation at a time. Ten concurrent requests need ten environments, which is why concurrency equals the number of environments in use. This also means global variables in one environment are not hit by two requests at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0019","card":"q-lambda-004-lambda-vs-ec2-and-the-execution-model","sec":"lambda-s005","q":"When would you choose containers on ECS or Fargate over Lambda?","a":"<p>Choose containers for long-running or always-on services, steady high traffic, workloads over 15 minutes or beyond Lambda's memory and package limits, and apps that need persistent connections or a specific runtime setup. Choose Lambda for event-driven, spiky or short work with no capacity management. Containers cost less at steady high utilization, while Lambda costs nothing when idle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0020","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What are the core components of an AWS Lambda function configuration?","a":"<p>Handler (entry point), runtime (execution environment), execution role (IAM permissions), memory (128 MB to 10,240 MB, which also sets CPU), timeout (up to 15 min), the deployment package (zip or container image), environment variables, trigger or event source config, concurrency settings, layers, ephemeral <code>/tmp</code> storage size and optional VPC config.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># SAM: the core configuration knobs in one place\nMyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    Handler: app.lambda_handler      # entry point (file.function)\n    Runtime: python3.12              # execution environment\n    MemorySize: 512                  # MB, also scales CPU/network\n    Timeout: 30                      # seconds (max 900)\n    Role: !GetAtt MyRole.Arn         # IAM execution role\n    EphemeralStorage: { Size: 512 }  # /tmp size in MB (512-10240)\n    Environment:\n      Variables: { TABLE_NAME: Users }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># The handler receives (event, context)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    return {&quot;statusCode&quot;: 200, &quot;body&quot;: &quot;ok&quot;}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0021","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"Which native runtimes does Lambda support out of the box?","a":"<p>Node.js, Python, Java, .NET (C#), Ruby and Go (via the provided runtime family). Anything else runs through Custom Runtimes using the Runtime API, or by packaging an OCI container image.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0022","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What is the max execution timeout for a single invocation?","a":"<p>900 seconds (15 minutes).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0023","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What is the maximum payload size for synchronous vs asynchronous calls?","a":"<p>Synchronous: 6 MB request/response. Asynchronous: 256 KB event payload.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0024","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"How does the pricing model work?","a":"<p>You pay for (a) the number of requests and (b) GB-seconds = allocated memory (GB) x execution duration (billed per 1 ms). More memory costs more per ms but can finish faster. There is a monthly free tier. Provisioned Concurrency and ephemeral storage above the default are billed separately.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0025","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"What is the default <code>/tmp</code> storage and its maximum?","a":"<p>Default 512 MB, configurable up to 10,240 MB (10 GB). It is scratch space that may persist across warm invocations but is not durable.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0026","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"In an AWS SAM template, what does the <code>Handler</code> property mean?","a":"<p>It names the entry point as <code>&lt;file&gt;.&lt;function&gt;</code>. Here Lambda imports <code>app.py</code> and calls its <code>lambda_handler(event, context)</code> function. The <code>Runtime</code> tells Lambda which language environment to start. A wrong handler string causes an import or handler-not-found error at invoke time.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Handler: app.lambda_handler\nRuntime: python3.12</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0027","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"How should you pick a timeout for a Lambda function behind API Gateway?","a":"<p>Set it slightly longer than the realistic worst case, and shorter than the caller's own timeout. API Gateway's integration timeout defaults to 29 seconds (HTTP APIs allow at most 30 s; since mid-2024 Regional REST APIs can raise it with a quota increase), so a function timeout far beyond that cannot reach the client anyway. A sane limit also caps cost when a downstream call hangs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0028","card":"q-lambda-005-configuration-runtimes-and-limits","sec":"lambda-s006","q":"Where does a Lambda function get its permissions to call other AWS services, and what is the default?","a":"<p>From its execution role. A function created in the console gets a role that only allows writing to CloudWatch Logs, so you add policies for every other service the code calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0029","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"Zip archives vs OCI container images?","a":"<p>Zip: upload code and dependencies as an archive (50 MB zipped for direct upload, 250 MB unzipped). Container image: an OCI image up to 10 GB, giving room for large dependencies or ML models and letting you use familiar container tooling. Zip is simpler and faster for small functions. Containers suit large or complex dependency sets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0030","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"What are the unzipped code and dependency size limits?","a":"<p>Zip deployment: 250 MB unzipped (code and layers combined). Direct zip upload: 50 MB, larger packages go via S3. Container image: up to 10 GB.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0031","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"What are Custom Runtimes, and how do you implement one with the Runtime API?","a":"<p>A custom runtime lets you run a language Lambda does not support natively. You provide a <code>bootstrap</code> executable that implements the Runtime API loop: poll <code>/runtime/invocation/next</code> for events, run your handler, and POST the result to <code>/runtime/invocation/{id}/response</code> (or <code>/error</code>). Ship it as a zip (on the <code>provided.al2023</code> or <code>provided.al2</code> base) or as a container image.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>#!/bin/sh\n# bootstrap: implements the Runtime API loop (provided.al2023)\nset -euo pipefail\nwhile true; do\n  # 1. Get the next invocation\n  EVENT=$(curl -sS &quot;http://$AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API/2018-06-01/runtime/invocation/next&quot; -D /tmp/h)\n  REQ_ID=$(grep -Fi Lambda-Runtime-Aws-Request-Id /tmp/h | tr -d &#x27;[:space:]&#x27; | cut -d: -f2)\n\n  # 2. Run your handler logic\n  RESPONSE=&quot;Hello from custom runtime: $EVENT&quot;\n\n  # 3. POST the result back\n  curl -sS &quot;http://$AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API/2018-06-01/runtime/invocation/$REQ_ID/response&quot; \\\n    -d &quot;$RESPONSE&quot;\ndone</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0032","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"When would you choose a container image over a zip package for Lambda?","a":"<p>When dependencies exceed the zip limits (for example ML libraries or models, up to 10 GB), when the team already builds and scans images in CI, or when you need a custom OS-level library. Zip stays simpler and starts quickly for small functions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0033","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"What must a container image contain to run on Lambda, and where is it stored?","a":"<p>The image must include a runtime that speaks the Lambda Runtime API, either an AWS-provided base image or your own image with the Runtime Interface Client added. The image is stored in Amazon ECR in the same region as the function.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0034","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"In a custom runtime's <code>bootstrap</code> script, what is the role of the <code>AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API</code> environment variable?","a":"<p>It holds the host and port of the local Runtime API endpoint inside the execution environment. The bootstrap loop calls <code>/runtime/invocation/next</code> to fetch the next event and then posts back to <code>/response</code> or <code>/error</code>. Without this loop, Lambda sees no handler activity.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>EVENT=$(curl -sS &quot;http://$AWS_LAMBDA_RUNTIME_API/2018-06-01/runtime/invocation/next&quot; -D /tmp/h)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0035","card":"q-lambda-006-packaging-zip-vs-container-image-and-cus","sec":"lambda-s007","q":"Why does a custom runtime need a file named <code>bootstrap</code>?","a":"<p>On the <code>provided</code> runtimes Lambda starts the executable called <code>bootstrap</code> in the package root and expects it to own the invocation loop. It must be executable. Compiled languages such as Rust or Go on <code>provided.al2023</code> ship the binary under this name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0036","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"How does Lambda scale under the hood when traffic spikes?","a":"<p>Lambda creates a new execution environment per concurrent request. When requests arrive faster than existing environments free up, it adds more, up to your concurrency limit. Scaling starts with an initial burst capacity, then continues at a controlled rate (for example 500 additional environments per function per minute in supported regions) until it reaches the account or function limit.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0037","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"What is Firecracker, and how does AWS use microVMs for Lambda?","a":"<p>Firecracker is AWS's open-source virtualization technology that launches lightweight microVMs in milliseconds. Lambda uses microVMs to give each execution environment strong VM-level isolation between functions and accounts while keeping startup fast and memory overhead tiny. It combines container-like speed with VM-like security.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0038","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"What is the difference between an event source mapping and a direct trigger?","a":"<p>A direct trigger is a service invoking Lambda by calling the Lambda API (push model), for example API Gateway, S3 or SNS. An event source mapping (ESM) is a Lambda-managed poller that pulls records from a source (SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams, Kafka), batches them and invokes your function. With an ESM, Lambda owns the polling loop. With a direct trigger, the source pushes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0039","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"Why did AWS build Lambda on Firecracker microVMs instead of plain containers?","a":"<p>Containers share the host kernel, so a kernel escape could cross tenants. MicroVMs give hardware-virtualized isolation per environment, and Firecracker keeps the start time in milliseconds with very small memory overhead. That makes it safe to run code from different customers on the same hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0040","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"In AWS Lambda, what happens to a function when 1,000 requests arrive at the same instant and no environments exist?","a":"<p>Lambda needs about 1,000 environments. It creates them within burst and scaling limits, each one a cold start. If the account limit or the scaling rate is hit, extra synchronous requests get a 429 throttle error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0041","card":"q-lambda-007-firecracker-and-scaling-under-the-hood","sec":"lambda-s008","q":"Why does Lambda's isolation model matter for security reviews?","a":"<p>Each execution environment is dedicated to one function version and is never shared across functions or accounts. You can state that tenants are separated by a VM boundary, not just process or container boundaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0042","card":"q-lambda-008-the-lambda-console","sec":"lambda-s009","q":"In AWS Lambda, you cannot find a function that a colleague mentioned. What do you check first?","a":"<p>The console region and the account. Lambda functions are regional and per account, so a function created in <code>eu-west-1</code> is invisible while the console is set to <code>us-east-1</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0043","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"In the Lambda console, what is the difference between saving code and clicking Deploy?","a":"<p>Deploy publishes your edited code to <code>$LATEST</code> so invocations use it. Testing before deploying runs the previous code, a common source of confusion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0044","card":"q-lambda-009-creating-and-testing-a-first-function","sec":"lambda-s010","q":"Do you need to configure a trigger before testing a Lambda function in the console?","a":"<p>No. The Test tab invokes the function directly with a JSON test event you supply. The event must look like what the real trigger would send, otherwise the test says little about production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0045","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"What are the arguments of a Lambda handler, and what does each carry?","a":"<p><code>event</code> is the trigger's data, in a shape that depends on the trigger. <code>context</code> holds runtime information such as function name, request ID and remaining time. In older Node.js style a <code>callback</code> returns the result, which <code>async</code> handlers do not need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0046","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"In a Node.js Lambda handler, what is the problem with mixing <code>async</code> and <code>callback</code>?","a":"<p>An <code>async</code> handler returns a Promise, and Lambda uses that Promise result. Calling <code>callback</code> as well is redundant and can lead to confusion about which result wins. Use either <code>return</code> in an <code>async</code> handler or the callback style in a non-async one, not both.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event, context, callback) =&gt; {\n    const response = { statusCode: 200, body: JSON.stringify(&#x27;Hello from Lambda!&#x27;) };\n    callback(null, response);\n};</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0047","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"Why do you parse the <code>event</code> defensively inside a Lambda handler?","a":"<p>Each trigger sends a different shape, optional fields can be missing (for example no query string) and bodies can be strings. Unchecked access throws, and the invocation fails. Logging the event once shows its real shape in CloudWatch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0048","card":"q-lambda-010-programming-model-trigger-handler-functi","sec":"lambda-s011","q":"In Python, what is the standard signature of a Lambda handler?","a":"<p>A function taking <code>(event, context)</code>. <code>event</code> is usually a dict, and the return value must be JSON-serializable. Its name is whatever the <code>Handler</code> setting points to (for example <code>app.lambda_handler</code>).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    return {&quot;statusCode&quot;: 200, &quot;body&quot;: &quot;ok&quot;}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0049","card":"q-lambda-011-the-three-execution-models-in-the-course","sec":"lambda-s012","q":"Why does returning a value from an S3-triggered Lambda function not matter?","a":"<p>S3 invokes Lambda asynchronously and does not read a response. Success or failure is signalled by the function finishing or throwing, so only errors and side effects matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0050","card":"q-lambda-011-the-three-execution-models-in-the-course","sec":"lambda-s012","q":"In Lambda, how does a poll-based source such as SQS differ from a push trigger such as S3 in who calls the function?","a":"<p>With SQS, a Lambda-managed poller reads the queue and invokes your function with batches, so Lambda needs read permission on the queue through the execution role. With S3, the service pushes an event and needs permission on the function's resource-based policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0051","card":"q-lambda-011-the-three-execution-models-in-the-course","sec":"lambda-s012","q":"A user clicks \"Place order\" and must see the result immediately. Which invocation type fits, and what would you do for a slow follow-up task?","a":"<p>Use synchronous invocation (API Gateway to Lambda) for the immediate response. For the slow follow-up, hand it to an asynchronous path such as an SQS queue or SNS topic so the user is not kept waiting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0052","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"In a Lambda function handling a Kinesis event, why is <code>data</code> decoded like this?","a":"<p>Kinesis delivers the record payload base64-encoded inside the event JSON. You decode it to get the original bytes or string, and usually parse that again as JSON.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const data = Buffer.from(record.kinesis.data, &#x27;base64&#x27;).toString();</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0053","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"In a DynamoDB Streams event delivered to Lambda, what do <code>eventName</code> and <code>NewImage</code> tell you?","a":"<p><code>eventName</code> is <code>INSERT</code>, <code>MODIFY</code> or <code>REMOVE</code>. <code>NewImage</code> is the item after the change, in DynamoDB attribute format where <code>{\"S\": \"123\"}</code> means a string. <code>OldImage</code> is available depending on the stream view type.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;Records&quot;: [ { &quot;eventName&quot;: &quot;INSERT&quot;, &quot;dynamodb&quot;: { &quot;NewImage&quot;: { &quot;id&quot;: { &quot;S&quot;: &quot;123&quot; } } } } ] }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0054","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"In Lambda, which permissions does a function need to consume from an SQS queue?","a":"<p>The execution role needs <code>sqs:ReceiveMessage</code>, <code>sqs:DeleteMessage</code> and <code>sqs:GetQueueAttributes</code> on that queue, because Lambda's poller acts with the function's role. The managed policy <code>AWSLambdaSQSQueueExecutionRole</code> grants them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0055","card":"q-lambda-012-common-triggers-and-their-event-shapes","sec":"lambda-s013","q":"In S3 event notifications, why can an S3-to-Lambda trigger cause an infinite loop, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>If the function writes its output into the same bucket and prefix that triggers it, each write fires the function again. Write to a different bucket or prefix, or filter the notification by prefix and suffix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0056","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"Why put API Gateway in front of Lambda instead of exposing the function directly?","a":"<p>Lambda has no public HTTP endpoint of its own for browsers and clients. API Gateway gives routing, authentication, throttling, caching, request validation, TLS with custom domains and usage tracking, so the function holds only business logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0057","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"In API Gateway, how do REST APIs and HTTP APIs differ, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>HTTP APIs are cheaper and faster with a simpler setup, with JWT authorizers and Lambda proxy support. REST APIs have more features: API keys and usage plans, request validation, caching, WAF integration and mapping templates. Choose HTTP API unless you need REST-only features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0058","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What is a Lambda authorizer in API Gateway, and what does it return?","a":"<p>It is a function API Gateway calls first to authenticate the request. It returns an IAM policy document that allows or denies <code>execute-api:Invoke</code> on the requested method (<code>methodArn</code>), plus a <code>principalId</code>. API Gateway enforces that policy and can cache it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return {\n    principalId: &#x27;user&#x27;,\n    policyDocument: {\n        Version: &#x27;2012-10-17&#x27;,\n        Statement: [{ Effect: &#x27;Allow&#x27;, Action: &#x27;execute-api:Invoke&#x27;, Resource: event.methodArn }]\n    }\n};</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0059","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"When is a Lambda function URL enough, and what does it lack compared with API Gateway?","a":"<p>A function URL is a built-in HTTPS endpoint for one function, with auth type <code>AWS_IAM</code> or <code>NONE</code>. It is enough for simple webhooks or internal callers and costs nothing extra. It has no throttling, usage plans or API keys, no Cognito/JWT or Lambda authorizers, no request validation or caching, and no custom domain without CloudFront. Use API Gateway when you need those features or several routes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0060","card":"q-lambda-013-amazon-api-gateway-as-a-trigger","sec":"lambda-s014","q":"What is the trade-off of integrating API Gateway directly with DynamoDB through VTL mapping templates instead of using a Lambda function?","a":"<p>You remove Lambda cost and latency and one moving part. In return, the logic lives in Velocity templates that are hard to test and debug, and anything beyond simple CRUD gets awkward. Use it for thin CRUD and keep Lambda for real business logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0061","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"In a Lambda function behind API Gateway, why is this line written with <code>|| {}</code>?","a":"<p>When the request has no query string, <code>event.queryStringParameters</code> is <code>null</code> or missing, and reading <code>.name</code> on it would throw a TypeError. Falling back to an empty object makes the later property access safe.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const queryParams = event.queryStringParameters || {};</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0062","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"Why is creating an API Gateway trigger with security set to \"Open\" a problem outside of experiments?","a":"<p>Anyone on the internet can call it, which exposes the function to abuse and cost. Use an authorizer (Cognito, JWT or Lambda), IAM auth or API keys with throttling, and add CORS only for known origins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0063","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"In API Gateway, what is a stage, and why does the invoke URL include one?","a":"<p>A stage is a named deployment of an API (for example <code>dev</code> or <code>prod</code>) with its own URL, settings, throttling and logging. It lets you run several versions of an API side by side and promote changes safely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0064","card":"q-lambda-014-adding-an-api-gateway-trigger-and-readin","sec":"lambda-s015","q":"A browser app calling your Lambda-backed API fails with a CORS error while Postman works. What is wrong?","a":"<p>Browsers enforce CORS, Postman does not. The API must answer the preflight <code>OPTIONS</code> request and return <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> (and related headers) for the calling origin. With Lambda proxy integration, the function must also add those headers to its response.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0065","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"In Lambda behind API Gateway, why does the handler call <code>JSON.parse(event.body)</code>?","a":"<p>With proxy integration, API Gateway delivers the request body as a string, whatever the content type. You parse it yourself. A malformed body makes <code>JSON.parse</code> throw, so wrap it in <code>try/catch</code> and return a 400 response.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if (!name &amp;&amp; event.body) {\n    const body = JSON.parse(event.body);\n    name = body.name || null;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0066","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"In API Gateway proxy events, what does <code>isBase64Encoded</code> mean for the body?","a":"<p>When true, the body is base64 text, which API Gateway uses for binary content. Decode it before use. For a response, setting it to true tells API Gateway to decode your base64 body to binary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0067","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"Why should a Lambda API return <code>400</code> for a bad request instead of <code>200</code> with an error message?","a":"<p>Status codes drive client behavior, caching, retries and monitoring. Returning 200 for errors hides failures from API Gateway metrics and alarms, and clients cannot tell success from failure without reading the body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0068","card":"q-lambda-015-testing-with-postman-and-handling-a-post","sec":"lambda-s016","q":"When testing an API during development, why use a tool such as Postman or curl instead of the browser address bar?","a":"<p>A browser address bar only sends simple <code>GET</code> requests. To test <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code> or <code>DELETE</code>, custom headers, JSON bodies and auth, you need a client that can build those requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0069","card":"q-lambda-016-challenge-add-two-numbers-from-a-post-re","sec":"lambda-s017","q":"In a Lambda handler that adds two query-string values, what is the pitfall of this check?","a":"<p>Query values arrive as strings, so you must convert them, and <code>parseInt</code> silently accepts partial input (<code>\"12abc\"</code> becomes 12) and drops decimals. Use <code>Number()</code> with a validity check if that matters, and give <code>parseInt</code> a radix. Invalid input should return a 400 status.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const num1 = parseInt(value1);\nconst num2 = parseInt(value2);\nif (!isNaN(num1) &amp;&amp; !isNaN(num2)) {</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0070","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"Walk through the lifecycle of an execution environment (Init, Invoke, Shutdown).","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Init</strong>: AWS creates the microVM, downloads code, starts the runtime and runs your initialization code (everything outside the handler). Extensions register here.</li>\n  <li><strong>Invoke</strong>: the handler runs for each event. A warm environment skips Init and goes straight here.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shutdown</strong>: when the environment is idle or reclaimed, Lambda sends a shutdown signal and extensions get a short window to clean up. Your handler is not called.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Python: code placement maps to the lifecycle\nimport boto3\n\n# --- INIT phase: runs ONCE per environment, reused across warm invokes ---\nddb = boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;)   # create clients here, not in the handler\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    # --- INVOKE phase: runs on EVERY event ---\n    return ddb.get_item(TableName=&quot;Users&quot;, Key={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: event[&quot;id&quot;]}})</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0071","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"What happens during Shutdown, and how can you use extension hooks?","a":"<p>When Lambda reclaims an idle environment, external extensions receive a <code>SHUTDOWN</code> event with a limited window (typically up to about 2 s) to flush logs and metrics, close connections or export telemetry. Use these hooks to clean up gracefully instead of losing buffered data.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0072","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"In AWS Lambda, what happens to the execution environment between two invocations, and what is the risk for background work?","a":"<p>Lambda freezes the environment after the handler returns and thaws it for the next invocation. Processes and callbacks that were still running are paused and may not finish, so finish all work, including <code>await</code> on promises, before returning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0073","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"In Lambda Python code, where would you place <code>boto3.client(\"dynamodb\")</code> and where the call to <code>get_item</code>, and why?","a":"<p>Create the client at module level, which runs in the Init phase once per environment, and call <code>get_item</code> inside the handler, which runs per event. Warm invocations then reuse the client and its connections.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0074","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"What are Lambda extensions, and how do they fit into the lifecycle?","a":"<p>Extensions are processes that run next to the function in the same environment, for example for monitoring, secrets caching or log shipping. They register at Init, can run during Invoke and receive a shutdown event to flush data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0075","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"Why should initialization code in Lambda avoid slow network calls that can fail?","a":"<p>Init runs on every cold start and delays the first request. If it throws, the invocation fails and the next cold start repeats the failure. Keep Init for cheap, reliable setup and defer optional or risky work to when it is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0076","card":"q-lambda-017-execution-environment-lifecycle","sec":"lambda-s018","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are the three phases of the execution environment lifecycle, and what runs in each?","a":"<p>Init, Invoke and Shutdown. In Init, Lambda starts extensions, starts the runtime and runs the function's code outside the handler (imports, client creation). In Invoke, the handler runs once per event, and warm environments repeat only this phase. In Shutdown, the runtime and extensions get a shutdown signal and a short time to flush data. Billing note: since August 2025 the Init phase is billed for on-demand functions on managed runtimes too; before that it was not billed for zip-packaged functions on managed runtimes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0077","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"What causes a cold start, and how does it affect latency?","a":"<p>A cold start happens when no warm environment is available, so Lambda must run the Init phase (spin up the microVM, load the runtime, run your init code) before invoking. This adds latency, from tens of ms to a few seconds depending on runtime and package size. Warm invocations skip it. Cold starts are most visible with spiky traffic, large packages, VPC-attached functions and heavy runtimes such as Java and .NET.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0078","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"What is Provisioned Concurrency, and how does it remove cold starts?","a":"<p>Provisioned Concurrency (PC) pre-initializes a set number of execution environments and keeps them warm, so invocations skip Init and get consistent low latency. You pay for the provisioned capacity whether it is used or not. It is often paired with Application Auto Scaling and used on a function alias or version.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-provisioned-concurrency-config \\\n  --function-name my-func --qualifier PROD \\\n  --provisioned-concurrent-executions 20</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0079","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"What is Lambda SnapStart, and how does it work for Java?","a":"<p>SnapStart (first for Java, now broader) snapshots the memory and disk of the initialized environment after Init, encrypts and caches it, and restores from that snapshot on invoke instead of re-running Init. It can cut Java cold starts sharply at no extra cost. Caveat: uniqueness. Anything created once at Init (random seeds, connection tokens) is captured in the snapshot, so use runtime hooks (<code>afterRestore</code>) to regenerate per-restore state.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Removes cold start?</th><th>Extra cost?</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Provisioned Concurrency</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (pre-warmed capacity)</td><td>Predictable, latency-sensitive traffic</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>SnapStart</td><td>Largely</td><td>No</td><td>Java (and supported runtimes)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Smaller package / lazy load</td><td>Reduces</td><td>No</td><td>Every function</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0080","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is a cold start and what happens during one compared with a warm start?","a":"<p>A cold start happens when no idle execution environment exists for the request. Lambda creates an environment, downloads the code, starts the runtime and runs the Init code outside the handler, and only then runs the handler. A warm start reuses an existing environment and runs just the handler, so it is much faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0081","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"Why is a scheduled \"keep warm\" ping a weak fix for cold starts compared with Provisioned Concurrency?","a":"<p>A ping keeps roughly one environment warm, but real traffic at higher concurrency still needs new environments, which are cold. Provisioned Concurrency keeps a configured number of environments initialized and scales with Application Auto Scaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0082","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"In Lambda, where does Provisioned Concurrency get configured, and can it use <code>$LATEST</code>?","a":"<p>It is configured on a published version or an alias, not on <code>$LATEST</code>. Pointing the alias at a new version moves the warm capacity with it during deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0083","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"A Lambda function has slow responses only for the first request after quiet periods. How do you confirm and reduce it?","a":"<p>Confirm with <code>Init Duration</code> in logs and X-Ray. Reduce Init work (smaller package, lazy imports, fewer or lighter dependencies), raise memory for faster init, choose a faster runtime, or use Provisioned Concurrency or SnapStart where the latency matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0084","card":"q-lambda-018-cold-starts-and-how-to-reduce-them","sec":"lambda-s019","q":"Does attaching a function to a VPC still cause large cold starts today?","a":"<p>No. Since the 2019 Hyperplane networking change, the network interfaces are created when the function is configured, not at invoke time, so the per-cold-start penalty is small.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0085","card":"q-lambda-019-package-size-and-runtime-choice","sec":"lambda-s020","q":"How does package size affect cold start duration?","a":"<p>Larger packages take longer to download and unpack during Init. Keep them lean: prune unused dependencies, use layers for shared libraries, tree-shake or minify, and avoid bundling large assets you do not need at runtime.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0086","card":"q-lambda-019-package-size-and-runtime-choice","sec":"lambda-s020","q":"How do compiled languages (Go, Rust) compare with interpreted ones (Python, Node.js) for cold starts?","a":"<p>Go and Rust usually have very fast cold starts and low memory overhead because they ship a native binary with no heavy runtime to boot. Python and Node.js are also fairly light. Java and .NET historically have the slowest cold starts because of JVM/CLR startup and class loading (mitigated by SnapStart and Provisioned Concurrency).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0087","card":"q-lambda-019-package-size-and-runtime-choice","sec":"lambda-s020","q":"Does moving dependencies into a Lambda layer make cold starts faster?","a":"<p>Not by itself. Layers are extracted into the same environment and still count towards the 250 MB unzipped limit. They help with sharing and deployment size, not initialization speed. Trimming what you load is what reduces cold starts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0088","card":"q-lambda-019-package-size-and-runtime-choice","sec":"lambda-s020","q":"In a Node.js Lambda project, how does a bundler such as esbuild help?","a":"<p>It bundles only the code you import (tree shaking) and minifies it into a few files. The package is smaller, so download and load during Init are faster, and you avoid shipping the whole <code>node_modules</code> folder.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0089","card":"q-lambda-019-package-size-and-runtime-choice","sec":"lambda-s020","q":"When would you accept Java on Lambda despite its slow cold starts?","a":"<p>When the team and libraries are already Java and the latency target can be met with SnapStart or Provisioned Concurrency. Warm Java invocations are fast, so the cold start cost matters only for spiky, latency-sensitive endpoints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0090","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"How does global variable initialization work between warm invocations?","a":"<p>Variables initialized outside the handler live in the execution environment and persist across warm invocations. That is how you cache SDK clients, DB connections and config. Do not rely on it for correctness (environments are transient), but use it for performance.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Node.js: cached across warm invokes\nconst cache = {};                    // survives between invocations\nexports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n  if (!cache.config) cache.config = await loadConfig(); // loaded once\n  return cache.config[event.key];\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0091","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"Why instantiate DB connections or SDK clients outside the handler?","a":"<p>Init runs once per environment and the objects are then reused across warm invocations. Creating a connection or client inside the handler rebuilds it on every request, adding latency and risking connection exhaustion.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0092","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"How do you reuse HTTP connection pools across invocations?","a":"<p>Create the HTTP client or agent outside the handler so keep-alive pools survive warm invocations. For the AWS SDK, keep-alive is the default in SDK v3 and is enabled by <code>AWS_NODEJS_CONNECTION_REUSE_ENABLED=1</code> in v2.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Node.js: reuse a keep-alive HTTP pool across invocations\nconst https = require(&quot;https&quot;);\nconst agent = new https.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 50 });\nexports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n  return callApi(event, { agent });  // pool persists while env is warm\n};</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0093","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"In Lambda, what does this caching code do, and what is its limitation?","a":"<p>It loads the config once per execution environment and reuses it on warm invocations, which saves a network call. Each environment has its own copy, and the data never refreshes until the environment is replaced, so changes to the config are seen late. Add a time-to-live if freshness matters.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const cache = {};\nexports.handler = async (event) =&gt; {\n  if (!cache.config) cache.config = await loadConfig();\n  return cache.config[event.key];\n};</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0094","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"Why is it dangerous to keep per-user data in a module-level variable in a Lambda function?","a":"<p>A warm environment serves many requests in sequence, so data left in a global from one user can leak into the next request. Keep globals for shared, read-only objects such as clients and config, and keep request data in local variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0095","card":"q-lambda-020-reusing-state-across-warm-invocations","sec":"lambda-s021","q":"In Lambda, how can <code>/tmp</code> be used safely as a cache?","a":"<p>Store downloaded files or models there and check whether the file exists before downloading again. Treat it as a best-effort cache, because a new environment starts empty. It is not shared between concurrent environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0096","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"How does memory affect CPU, network bandwidth and cost?","a":"<p>Memory is the one dial that also scales CPU and network proportionally. More memory means more vCPU and bandwidth and often faster execution. Cost per ms rises with memory, but faster completion can lower total GB-seconds, so tuning memory can reduce both latency and cost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0097","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"What tool tunes a function's memory (for example AWS Lambda Power Tuning)?","a":"<p>AWS Lambda Power Tuning is a Step Functions state machine that runs the function at several memory settings and charts cost against speed so you pick the sweet spot. You can also compare CloudWatch <code>REPORT</code> logs (Max Memory Used, Duration) across settings.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0098","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"How does lazy vs eager loading of libraries affect execution time?","a":"<p>Eager loading (importing everything at Init) lengthens cold starts but makes every invoke fast. Lazy loading (importing a heavy module only when a code path needs it) shortens cold starts and avoids paying for libraries you rarely use, at the cost of a one-time hit on the first invoke that needs them. Choose by how often each path runs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Lazy load a heavy dependency only when needed\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    if event.get(&quot;needs_pandas&quot;):\n        import pandas as pd          # imported only on this path\n        return process(pd, event)\n    return quick_path(event)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0099","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"A CPU-heavy Lambda function takes 8 s at 512 MB. Raising memory to 2048 MB cuts it to 2 s. What happens to cost and latency?","a":"<p>Memory is 4x larger and the duration is 4x shorter, so GB-seconds stay the same. The cost is about equal and latency is much better. If the speedup is larger than the memory increase, the bill goes down. That is why measuring across several memory sizes is worthwhile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0100","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"In this Python Lambda handler, what is the benefit and the cost of the import location?","a":"<p>The heavy <code>pandas</code> import runs only on requests that need it, so cold starts and common requests stay fast. The first request that needs it pays the import time. Imports inside a function are cached after the first time within that environment.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    if event.get(&quot;needs_pandas&quot;):\n        import pandas as pd\n        return process(pd, event)\n    return quick_path(event)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0101","card":"q-lambda-021-tuning-memory-and-lazy-loading","sec":"lambda-s022","q":"How do you find out whether a Lambda function is over-provisioned on memory?","a":"<p>Compare <code>Max Memory Used</code> with <code>Memory Size</code> in the REPORT lines or CloudWatch Lambda Insights. If usage is far under the setting and duration does not improve with more memory, lower it. If the function is CPU-bound, test higher settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0102","card":"q-lambda-022-latency-fixes-at-a-glance","sec":"lambda-s023","q":"Users report that an API backed by Lambda is slow. What is your diagnosis order?","a":"<p>Check whether latency comes from cold starts (<code>Init Duration</code>), the handler itself or a downstream call. Use X-Ray or logs to find the slow segment, then act: increase memory for CPU-bound work, reuse clients and connections, trim imports, use Provisioned Concurrency or SnapStart for cold starts, and fix slow dependencies or add caching.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0103","card":"q-lambda-022-latency-fixes-at-a-glance","sec":"lambda-s023","q":"In AWS Lambda, why enable X-Ray tracing before changing any configuration?","a":"<p>Tracing shows where time goes: initialization, the function and each downstream call (DynamoDB, HTTP). Without it, tuning memory or concurrency may fix the wrong part.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0104","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"What are the three invocation models?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Synchronous</strong>: the caller invokes and waits for the response (<code>RequestResponse</code>). Errors return to the caller.</li>\n  <li><strong>Asynchronous</strong>: the caller hands the event to Lambda's internal queue and gets an immediate acknowledgment (<code>Event</code>). Lambda invokes and handles retries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Event Source Mapping (ESM)</strong>: Lambda polls a source (SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams, Kafka), batches records and invokes the function.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda invoke --function-name f --invocation-type RequestResponse out.json  # sync\naws lambda invoke --function-name f --invocation-type Event out.json            # async</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0105","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"Three services that invoke Lambda synchronously?","a":"<p>API Gateway, Application Load Balancer (ALB) and a direct <code>Invoke</code> call from the SDK or CLI. Also Cognito, Lex and Step Functions optimized <code>Task</code> states.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0106","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"Three services that invoke Lambda asynchronously?","a":"<p>S3 event notifications, SNS and EventBridge (scheduled or rule-based). Also CloudWatch Logs, SES and CodeCommit.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0107","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"What is an ESM, and which services use it?","a":"<p>An ESM is a Lambda-managed resource that polls a stream or queue and invokes your function with batches. It is used by SQS, Kinesis Data Streams, DynamoDB Streams, Amazon MSK or self-managed Kafka, and Amazon MQ. Lambda owns the polling, batching, checkpointing and scaling of pollers.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0108","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"In Lambda, which HTTP status codes do a successful synchronous invoke and an asynchronous invoke return from the Invoke API?","a":"<p>A synchronous invoke returns 200 with the function's response. An asynchronous invoke returns 202 straight away, which only means Lambda queued the event.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0109","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"In the AWS CLI, what does <code>--invocation-type DryRun</code> do for <code>aws lambda invoke</code>?","a":"<p>It validates the parameters and permissions without running the function. It returns 204 when the call would be accepted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0110","card":"q-lambda-023-synchronous-asynchronous-and-event-sourc","sec":"lambda-s024","q":"Your webhook provider retries if no answer arrives within 5 seconds, but your processing takes 30 seconds. How would you design the Lambda side?","a":"<p>Receive the webhook in a function that validates and stores or enqueues the payload (SQS) and replies quickly. A second function processes the queue. Use idempotency keys, because the provider may retry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0111","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"What happens when an asynchronous invocation fails?","a":"<p>Lambda retries automatically twice (3 attempts in total) with delays. If it still fails, the event goes to the configured <strong>Dead Letter Queue</strong> (SQS or SNS) or <strong>On-failure Destination</strong>. Tune <code>MaximumRetryAttempts</code> (0 to 2) and <code>MaximumEventAge</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-function-event-invoke-config --function-name f \\\n  --maximum-retry-attempts 1 --maximum-event-age-in-seconds 3600 \\\n  --destination-config &#x27;{&quot;OnFailure&quot;:{&quot;Destination&quot;:&quot;arn:aws:sqs:...:dlq&quot;}}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0112","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"DLQ vs Lambda Destinations?","a":"<p>A DLQ (SQS or SNS) captures only the <strong>failed event payload</strong> after async retries run out. <strong>Destinations</strong> route on <strong>success or failure</strong> and send full context (request plus response or error) to SQS, SNS, EventBridge or another Lambda. They are the modern, more informative option for async invocations.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0113","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"When use Destinations over SQS/SNS DLQs?","a":"<p>Prefer Destinations when you want success routing, full invocation context (not just the payload) or workflow chaining. Use a plain DLQ when you only need failed payloads for later reprocessing and want the simplest setup. A DLQ applies to async invocations and to ESM sources. Destinations apply to async invocations and to stream ESMs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0114","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"Because Lambda retries failed asynchronous invocations, what must your code guarantee?","a":"<p>Idempotency. The same event may be processed more than once, and S3 and EventBridge also deliver at least once. Use a unique event ID or business key and check or record it, for example with a DynamoDB conditional write.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0115","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"What are the default retry timings for a failed async Lambda invocation?","a":"<p>Two retries by default, about 1 minute after the first failure and 2 minutes after the second, so three attempts in total. <code>MaximumRetryAttempts</code> (0 to 2) and <code>MaximumEventAge</code> (up to 6 hours) change that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0116","card":"q-lambda-024-async-failures-retries-and-dead-letter-h","sec":"lambda-s025","q":"Why would an on-failure destination fail to receive events even though it is configured?","a":"<p>The function's execution role needs permission to send to the target (<code>sqs:SendMessage</code>, <code>sns:Publish</code>, <code>events:PutEvents</code> or <code>lambda:InvokeFunction</code>). Without it the failure delivery is dropped, so check the role and the target's policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0117","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"How does Lambda poll SQS, and what are batch size vs batch window?","a":"<p>Lambda runs pollers that call <code>ReceiveMessage</code> and invoke your function with a batch. <strong>Batch size</strong> is the maximum records per invocation (up to 10,000 for standard SQS, within a 6 MB payload). <strong>Batch window</strong> (<code>MaximumBatchingWindowInSeconds</code>, 0 to 300) is how long Lambda waits to gather a fuller batch before invoking. Successful batches are deleted from the queue automatically. Failures return to the queue after the visibility timeout.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Events:\n  FromQueue:\n    Type: SQS\n    Properties:\n      Queue: !GetAtt MyQueue.Arn\n      BatchSize: 10\n      MaximumBatchingWindowInSeconds: 20\n      FunctionResponseTypes: [ReportBatchItemFailures]   # partial batch success</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0118","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"How does Maximum Concurrency work with SQS as an event source?","a":"<p><code>MaximumConcurrency</code> (2 to 1000) on the SQS ESM caps how many concurrent invocations the poller drives. It protects downstream systems and stops a busy queue from consuming all account concurrency, without reserved concurrency (which throttled the pollers awkwardly).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Events:\n  FromQueue:\n    Type: SQS\n    Properties:\n      Queue: !GetAtt MyQueue.Arn\n      ScalingConfig: { MaximumConcurrency: 50 }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0119","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"In a Lambda function that processes SQS batches, how do you report only the failed messages?","a":"<p>Enable <code>ReportBatchItemFailures</code> on the event source mapping and return <code>batchItemFailures</code> with the <code>messageId</code> of each failed message. Lambda deletes the successes and returns only those failures to the queue. Without it, one bad message makes the whole batch retry.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    failures = []\n    for record in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        try:\n            handle(record[&quot;body&quot;])\n        except Exception:\n            failures.append({&quot;itemIdentifier&quot;: record[&quot;messageId&quot;]})\n    return {&quot;batchItemFailures&quot;: failures}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0120","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"Where do you configure the dead letter queue for messages that keep failing from an SQS-triggered function?","a":"<p>On the source SQS queue, as a redrive policy with <code>maxReceiveCount</code> and a target DLQ. The Lambda function's own DLQ setting applies to asynchronous invocations, not to queue polling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0121","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"In a Lambda SQS event source mapping, what do batch size and batching window do, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>Batch size is the maximum number of messages per invocation. The batching window is how long Lambda waits to fill a batch before invoking. Larger batches and longer windows mean fewer invocations and lower cost, but higher latency, and when one message fails without partial batch responses the whole larger batch is retried. Standard queues allow a window up to 5 minutes, and the batch is also capped by the 6 MB invocation payload.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0122","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"How does Lambda scale an SQS-triggered function, and what does <code>MaximumConcurrency</code> limit?","a":"<p>Lambda's pollers add concurrent invocations as the queue backlog grows, so a large queue can drive many parallel functions. <code>MaximumConcurrency</code> on the event source mapping caps how many concurrent invocations that queue can trigger (minimum 2), which protects downstream systems without throttling and returning messages to the queue. Reserved concurrency on the function is the worse way to cap it: invocations beyond the limit are throttled, the messages return to the queue, and they may reach the DLQ after <code>maxReceiveCount</code>. Prefer <code>MaximumConcurrency</code> on the mapping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0123","card":"q-lambda-025-sqs-event-source-batching-and-concurrenc","sec":"lambda-s026","q":"How does a FIFO SQS queue change the way Lambda processes messages compared with a standard queue?","a":"<p>A FIFO queue keeps order within a message group ID. Lambda processes one batch per message group at a time, so concurrency is bounded by the number of active message groups and a failed message blocks later messages in the same group until it succeeds or goes to the DLQ. That makes FIFO scale more slowly than a standard queue, which gives high parallelism but at-least-once delivery with no ordering guarantee. Use FIFO when ordering or deduplication matters, and standard when throughput matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0124","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"How does Lambda handle Kinesis or DynamoDB Streams?","a":"<p>Lambda reads records per shard in order, one batch at a time, and checkpoints on success. Concurrency is tied to shards: by default one batch per shard at a time, and <code>ParallelizationFactor</code> (up to 10) allows several concurrent batches per shard while keeping per-partition-key order. A failing batch blocks that shard until it succeeds or is discarded under your error-handling config.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0125","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"How does Lambda handle poison pill records in stream processing?","a":"<p>A poison pill is a record that always fails and blocks the shard. Mitigations: set <code>MaximumRetryAttempts</code> and <code>MaximumRecordAgeInSeconds</code> so it is eventually discarded, enable <code>BisectBatchOnFunctionError</code> to isolate it, and configure an <strong>on-failure destination</strong> to capture discarded records instead of losing them.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0126","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"What is Bisect on Function Error?","a":"<p><code>BisectBatchOnFunctionError</code> splits a failing batch in half and retries each half, narrowing down until it isolates the single offending record. Good records succeed while only the poison record is retried or discarded, instead of failing the whole batch.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0127","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"Why can one bad record stall a Kinesis-triggered Lambda function for a long time?","a":"<p>Streams keep order per shard, so Lambda retries a failing batch until it succeeds or the records expire. Later records on that shard wait behind it. Limit retries and record age, bisect the batch and send failures to an on-failure destination.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0128","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"In a Lambda event source mapping for Kinesis, what does the starting position <code>TRIM_HORIZON</code> do compared with <code>LATEST</code>?","a":"<p><code>TRIM_HORIZON</code> starts from the oldest record still in the stream, and <code>LATEST</code> reads only new records after the mapping is created. Use <code>TRIM_HORIZON</code> when you must not miss existing data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0129","card":"q-lambda-026-stream-processing-poison-pills-and-bisec","sec":"lambda-s027","q":"How do you raise throughput for a Lambda consumer of a stream with a fixed number of shards?","a":"<p>Increase <code>ParallelizationFactor</code> (up to 10 concurrent batches per shard), tune batch size and window, speed up the function, or add shards. Per-key order is kept within a shard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0130","card":"q-lambda-027-api-gateway-integrations-and-scheduling","sec":"lambda-s028","q":"API Gateway Proxy Integration vs Custom Integration?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Proxy (Lambda proxy) integration</strong>: API Gateway passes the entire raw request (path, headers, query, body) to Lambda as the standard <code>event</code>, and Lambda must return a <code>{statusCode, headers, body}</code> shape. Simple, flexible, minimal mapping config.</li>\n  <li><strong>Custom (non-proxy) integration</strong>: you use mapping templates (VTL) to transform the request and response between client and Lambda. More control and decoupling, more configuration.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Proxy integration must return this exact shape\nexports.handler = async (event) =&gt; ({\n  statusCode: 200,\n  headers: { &quot;Content-Type&quot;: &quot;application/json&quot; },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ msg: `Hello ${event.queryStringParameters?.name ?? &quot;world&quot;}` }),\n});</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0131","card":"q-lambda-027-api-gateway-integrations-and-scheduling","sec":"lambda-s028","q":"How do you trigger a function on a schedule?","a":"<p>Use Amazon EventBridge (Scheduler or a rule) with a <code>rate()</code> or <code>cron()</code> expression. EventBridge invokes the function asynchronously.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Events:\n  EveryFiveMinutes:\n    Type: Schedule\n    Properties:\n      Schedule: rate(5 minutes)        # or cron(0 12 * * ? *)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0132","card":"q-lambda-027-api-gateway-integrations-and-scheduling","sec":"lambda-s028","q":"A Lambda proxy integration returns an API Gateway 502 \"Malformed Lambda proxy response\". What is the likely cause?","a":"<p>The function returned something other than the expected shape, for example a plain string or an object without <code>statusCode</code>, or a <code>body</code> that is not a string. Return <code>{statusCode, headers, body}</code> with a string body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0133","card":"q-lambda-027-api-gateway-integrations-and-scheduling","sec":"lambda-s028","q":"In EventBridge, what does <code>cron(0 12 * * ? *)</code> mean?","a":"<p>Run every day at 12:00 UTC. The fields are minutes, hours, day-of-month, month, day-of-week and year, and one of the day fields must be <code>?</code>. Schedules run in UTC unless you use EventBridge Scheduler with a time zone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0134","card":"q-lambda-027-api-gateway-integrations-and-scheduling","sec":"lambda-s028","q":"What is the difference between <code>rate(5 minutes)</code> and a <code>cron()</code> expression for scheduling Lambda?","a":"<p><code>rate()</code> runs at a fixed interval after creation. <code>cron()</code> runs at specific calendar times such as weekdays at 09:00. Use <code>rate()</code> for simple periodic polling and <code>cron()</code> for clock-bound jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0135","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"Why should you avoid using the AWS root user for daily work, and what should you protect it with?","a":"<p>Root has unrestricted access that cannot be limited by IAM policies, so a leaked credential means total compromise. Enable MFA, remove root access keys, keep the credentials in a vault, and use IAM users or roles with least privilege for daily tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0136","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"In IAM policy evaluation, what happens when one policy allows an action and another explicitly denies it?","a":"<p>The explicit deny wins. By default everything is denied, an allow is needed to permit an action, and an explicit deny always overrides any allow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0137","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"In this IAM policy, what is too broad?","a":"<p><code>Resource: \"*\"</code> grants read and list access to every bucket in the account. Scope it to only the buckets and objects the function needs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n  &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;, &quot;s3:ListBucket&quot;],\n  &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0138","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What are the main elements of an IAM policy statement?","a":"<p><code>Effect</code> (Allow or Deny), <code>Action</code> (the API calls), <code>Resource</code> (the ARNs the actions apply to) and optionally <code>Principal</code> (for resource-based policies) and <code>Condition</code>. <code>Version</code> states the policy language, normally <code>2012-10-17</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0139","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"Why attach IAM permissions to groups instead of directly to users?","a":"<p>A group applies the same permissions to every member, so adding or removing a person is one change and permissions stay consistent and auditable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0140","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"What is the difference between an IAM role and an IAM user, and which should a Lambda function use?","a":"<p>A user has long-lived credentials for one person or application. A role has no permanent credentials and is assumed by a trusted principal, which receives temporary credentials. A Lambda function uses an execution role, so no access keys are stored in code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0141","card":"q-lambda-028-root-user-iam-users-and-policies","sec":"lambda-s029","q":"In IAM, how do AWS managed policies, customer managed policies and inline policies differ, and which should you prefer?","a":"<p>AWS managed policies are created and updated by AWS and are often broad. Customer managed policies are yours, reusable across identities and versioned. Inline policies are embedded in one identity and are deleted with it. Prefer customer managed policies written to least privilege, and use inline ones only for a strict one-to-one relationship. A permissions boundary can additionally cap the maximum permissions an identity may receive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0142","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"Why is attaching <code>AdministratorAccess</code> to a developer's user a bad default?","a":"<p>It allows every action on every resource, so a leaked key or a mistake can delete or expose anything. Give developers only the permissions they need, or use temporary role-based access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0143","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"Where does <code>aws configure</code> store credentials, and what are the risks?","a":"<p>In <code>~/.aws/credentials</code> (keys) and <code>~/.aws/config</code> (region and output). They are long-lived plain text, so never commit them, restrict file access and rotate regularly. Prefer IAM Identity Center or role assumption, which gives short-lived credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0144","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"How do you check which identity the AWS CLI is currently using?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws sts get-caller-identity</code>. It prints the account ID, user or role ARN and user ID, which helps when a command fails with AccessDenied.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0145","card":"q-lambda-029-iam-users-groups-and-cli-credentials","sec":"lambda-s030","q":"Why are IAM access keys discouraged for applications running inside AWS?","a":"<p>Code running on Lambda, EC2 or ECS should use its role, which provides temporary credentials that rotate automatically. Static keys can leak and need manual rotation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0146","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"What is the execution role, and what is a resource-based policy?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Execution role</strong> (identity-based, the IAM role the function assumes): defines what the function's code may do to <em>other</em> AWS services (read a DynamoDB table, write to S3). This is <em>outbound</em> permission.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resource-based policy</strong> (attached to the function): defines <em>who</em> may <em>invoke</em> the function (for example \"allow S3 bucket X to invoke me\"). This is <em>inbound</em> permission.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Principal&quot;: {&quot;Service&quot;: &quot;s3.amazonaws.com&quot;},\n  &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;lambda:InvokeFunction&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:...:function:f&quot;,\n  &quot;Condition&quot;: {&quot;ArnLike&quot;: {&quot;AWS:SourceArn&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket&quot;}} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0147","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"How do you give a function access to an S3 bucket?","a":"<p>Attach an IAM policy to the function's <strong>execution role</strong> that grants the needed S3 actions on the specific bucket or prefix (least privilege).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;, &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n  &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n  &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;, &quot;s3:PutObject&quot;],\n  &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*&quot;\n}]}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0148","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"What is least privilege for Lambda IAM roles?","a":"<p>Grant the execution role only the exact actions on the exact resources the function needs. No <code>Action: \"*\"</code> or <code>Resource: \"*\"</code>. Scope to specific ARNs and prefixes and use conditions. This limits the blast radius if the function or its credentials are compromised.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0149","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"In Lambda, which policy do you change when an S3 bucket cannot invoke the function, and which when the function cannot write to DynamoDB?","a":"<p>For the bucket, the function's resource-based policy must allow the S3 principal to invoke it. For DynamoDB writes, the execution role needs <code>dynamodb:PutItem</code> on the table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0150","card":"q-lambda-030-execution-role-vs-resource-based-policy","sec":"lambda-s031","q":"How would you grant another AWS service permission to invoke a Lambda function from the CLI?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws lambda add-permission</code> with a statement ID, <code>--action lambda:InvokeFunction</code>, the service as <code>--principal</code> and a <code>--source-arn</code>. This adds a statement to the resource-based policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0151","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"How should sensitive credentials or API keys reach a function?","a":"<p>Never hard-code them. Store them in <strong>AWS Secrets Manager</strong> or <strong>SSM Parameter Store (SecureString)</strong>, grant the execution role read access, and fetch at Init (cache in a global). The Secrets/Parameter Store Lambda extension can cache values and reduce API calls.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3, os\n_secret = None\ndef get_secret():\n    global _secret\n    if _secret is None:                      # fetch once per environment\n        _secret = boto3.client(&quot;secretsmanager&quot;) \\\n            .get_secret_value(SecretId=os.environ[&quot;SECRET_ARN&quot;])[&quot;SecretString&quot;]\n    return _secret</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0152","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"Environment variables vs Secrets Manager / SSM Parameter Store?","a":"<p>Env vars are simple key/values stored with the function config. They suit non-secret config (table names, flags) and are encrypted at rest with KMS. Secrets Manager and SSM are built for secrets: centralized, access-controlled, auditable and they support <strong>rotation</strong>. Sensitive values belong there, not in env vars, which are visible to anyone with <code>GetFunctionConfiguration</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0153","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"How do you encrypt env vars at rest and in transit?","a":"<p>At rest Lambda encrypts them with a KMS key (AWS-managed by default; use a customer-managed CMK for tighter control and audit). For transit and console protection, enable <strong>encryption helpers</strong> so values are encrypted client-side with your CMK and decrypted in code at runtime via KMS.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0154","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"In this Lambda Python code, why is the secret kept in a global, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>It fetches the secret once per execution environment, so warm invocations avoid an API call and its cost. If the secret is rotated, warm environments keep the old value until they restart, so add a refresh interval or retry on authentication failure.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3, os\n_secret = None\ndef get_secret():\n    global _secret\n    if _secret is None:\n        _secret = boto3.client(&quot;secretsmanager&quot;) \\\n            .get_secret_value(SecretId=os.environ[&quot;SECRET_ARN&quot;])[&quot;SecretString&quot;]\n    return _secret</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0155","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"What extra permission does a Lambda function need when a secret or parameter is encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key?","a":"<p><code>kms:Decrypt</code> on that key, in the execution role and allowed by the key policy, in addition to <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> or <code>ssm:GetParameter</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0156","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store SecureString: how do you choose?","a":"<p>Secrets Manager has built-in rotation and cross-region replication but costs per secret. Parameter Store is cheaper (the standard tier is free) and good for config and simple secrets, with no native rotation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0157","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"What is the total size limit of Lambda environment variables?","a":"<p>4 KB for all variables together. Keep large configuration in Parameter Store, AppConfig or S3.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0158","card":"q-lambda-031-secrets-environment-variables-and-encryp","sec":"lambda-s032","q":"How are Lambda environment variables encrypted, and why should secrets not rely on that alone?","a":"<p>They are encrypted at rest with an AWS managed key by default, or with a customer-managed KMS key if you choose one. Lambda decrypts them when it starts the environment, so the code sees plain text. Anyone with <code>lambda:GetFunctionConfiguration</code> can read them in the console or API, and they can leak through logs or crash dumps. Keep real secrets in Secrets Manager or Parameter Store and read them at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0159","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"How do Lambda Authorizers work with API Gateway?","a":"<p>A Lambda Authorizer is a function API Gateway calls before the target function to authenticate and authorize a request. <strong>Token</strong> authorizers inspect a bearer token. <strong>Request</strong> authorizers inspect headers, query and context. It returns an IAM policy (Allow or Deny) plus optional context, and API Gateway caches the result by key and TTL.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>exports.handler = async (event) =&gt; ({\n  principalId: &quot;user123&quot;,\n  policyDocument: { Version: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;, Statement: [{\n    Action: &quot;execute-api:Invoke&quot;,\n    Effect: isValid(event.authorizationToken) ? &quot;Allow&quot; : &quot;Deny&quot;,\n    Resource: event.methodArn }] },\n  context: { role: &quot;admin&quot; },   // passed to the backend function\n});</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0160","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"How do you stop an arbitrary S3 bucket from triggering your function?","a":"<p>On the function's <strong>resource-based policy</strong>, scope the S3 principal with a <code>SourceArn</code> condition (the specific bucket ARN) and <code>SourceAccount</code>. Only that bucket can invoke the function, which prevents a confused-deputy case where another bucket or account triggers it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0161","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"Can a function in Account A be triggered by an S3 event in Account B?","a":"<p>Yes. Add a resource-based policy on the Account A function that allows the S3 service principal with <code>SourceAccount</code> = Account B and <code>SourceArn</code> = the Account B bucket. Configure the Account B bucket notification to point at the Account A function ARN. Cross-account access needs explicit permissions on both sides.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0162","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"A Lambda authorizer result is cached for 300 seconds, and users start getting 403 on routes they should access. What is likely wrong?","a":"<p>The cached policy only names the one <code>methodArn</code> it was created for, so another route reuses a policy that does not allow it. Return a policy that covers all the routes the caller may use (for example a wildcard on the API's stage) or disable caching.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0163","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"In API Gateway, when would you choose a Cognito or JWT authorizer instead of a Lambda authorizer?","a":"<p>When tokens come from Cognito or another OIDC provider, a built-in authorizer validates them with no code to write or run. Use a Lambda authorizer for custom logic such as API keys in a database, opaque tokens or tenant rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0164","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"How can API Gateway authorize callers by AWS identity rather than by token?","a":"<p>Enable IAM authorization on the method. Clients sign requests with SigV4 and their IAM policy must allow <code>execute-api:Invoke</code>. It suits service-to-service calls inside AWS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0165","card":"q-lambda-032-authorizers-resource-policies-and-cross","sec":"lambda-s033","q":"In a cross-account setup, why is an IAM role in one account alone not enough for access to a function in another?","a":"<p>Cross-account access needs permission on both sides: the caller's identity policy allows the action, and the target's resource-based policy allows that principal.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0166","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"How do code signing and AWS Signer prevent unauthorized code?","a":"<p>With <strong>Code Signing for Lambda</strong> you attach a signing profile (AWS Signer). Lambda verifies the package signature against trusted publishers before accepting it, and rejects unsigned, tampered or expired packages. Only trusted, unmodified code gets deployed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0167","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"What is cross-account deployment for Lambda?","a":"<p>Deploying or updating a function's code or infrastructure from another account, for example a central CI/CD account deploying into dev and prod accounts. It is usually done with cross-account IAM roles (the pipeline assumes a role in the target account), shared artifact buckets or ECR with cross-account policies, and CloudFormation/SAM StackSets.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0168","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"How do you isolate tenants in a multi-tenant serverless system?","a":"<p>Along a spectrum: a separate AWS account per tenant (strongest isolation), or shared functions with per-tenant IAM scoping using <strong>session tags or dynamic policies</strong> (<code>sts:AssumeRole</code> with tenant context, ABAC conditions on <code>${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant}</code>), per-tenant resource prefixes or partition keys, and reserved concurrency per tenant to prevent noisy neighbors. Choose by isolation against cost and operational overhead.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0169","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"In Lambda code signing, what is the difference between the <code>Warn</code> and <code>Enforce</code> policies?","a":"<p>A code signing configuration lists the allowed signing profiles. With <code>Warn</code> Lambda accepts an unsigned or untrusted package and logs a warning, and with <code>Enforce</code> it rejects the deployment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0170","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"In a multi-tenant Lambda system, how does reserved concurrency help isolate tenants?","a":"<p>It caps each tenant's function so one tenant cannot use the whole account concurrency and starve others. It requires separate functions or aliases per tenant.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0171","card":"q-lambda-033-code-signing-cross-account-deployment-an","sec":"lambda-s034","q":"How would a central CI/CD account deploy a Lambda function into a production account?","a":"<p>The pipeline assumes a deployment role in the production account (trusted by the pipeline account), and runs CloudFormation or SAM using it. Artifacts sit in a bucket or ECR repository with a policy that lets the target account read them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0172","card":"q-lambda-034-attaching-a-function-to-a-vpc","sec":"lambda-s035","q":"What happens when a function is attached to a VPC?","a":"<p>The function can reach private VPC resources (RDS, ElastiCache, internal services) through Elastic Network Interfaces. It then follows VPC routing, so it loses default internet access unless you add a NAT gateway (private subnets) or a VPC endpoint. Thanks to AWS Hyperplane, ENIs are shared and set up at function create or update time, so VPC attachment no longer adds a per-invoke cold-start penalty.</p>\n<p>Without a VPC, functions run in an AWS-managed network with internet access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0173","card":"q-lambda-034-attaching-a-function-to-a-vpc","sec":"lambda-s035","q":"What is an ENI, and how does Hyperplane optimize VPC access?","a":"<p>An ENI is a virtual network card that gives the function an IP inside your VPC. Historically Lambda created an ENI per concurrent environment (slow and IP-hungry). <strong>Hyperplane</strong> now creates shared, network-mapped ENIs per function, subnet and security-group combination at create or update time and multiplexes many execution environments through them. That removes per-invocation ENI setup and its cold-start cost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0174","card":"q-lambda-034-attaching-a-function-to-a-vpc","sec":"lambda-s035","q":"When should you attach a Lambda function to a VPC, and when not?","a":"<p>Attach it only when it must reach private resources such as RDS or ElastiCache. Otherwise leave it out, because a VPC-attached function loses default internet access and needs NAT or endpoints, adding cost and configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0175","card":"q-lambda-034-attaching-a-function-to-a-vpc","sec":"lambda-s035","q":"Why should a VPC-attached Lambda function use subnets in several Availability Zones?","a":"<p>If one zone fails, Lambda can still run the function in the others, which keeps it available. Each subnet needs enough free IP addresses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0176","card":"q-lambda-034-attaching-a-function-to-a-vpc","sec":"lambda-s035","q":"A Lambda function in a VPC suddenly times out on every call to an external API. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether the subnets are private with a route to a NAT gateway, whether the NAT gateway is healthy, the route table, security group egress and network ACLs. A function in a VPC has no internet access without NAT.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0177","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"How can a VPC-bound function reach the public internet?","a":"<p>Place the function in <strong>private</strong> subnets and route <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> through a <strong>NAT gateway</strong> in a public subnet, which reaches the internet via an Internet Gateway. A function in a public subnet cannot reach the internet directly because it has no public IP. For AWS services without internet, use VPC endpoints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0178","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"How do VPC Endpoints (PrivateLink) let a private function reach AWS services?","a":"<p>VPC endpoints keep traffic on the AWS private network. <strong>Gateway endpoints</strong> (S3, DynamoDB) add a route-table entry. <strong>Interface endpoints (PrivateLink)</strong> create an ENI with a private IP for services such as Secrets Manager, SQS and KMS. A private, no-NAT function can then reach those services securely and often more cheaply.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0179","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"Why can a Lambda function in a public subnet not reach the internet?","a":"<p>Lambda network interfaces only get private IP addresses, so an Internet Gateway route is not enough. Place the function in a private subnet with a route to a NAT gateway located in a public subnet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0180","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"When would you use a VPC endpoint instead of a NAT gateway for Lambda access to AWS services?","a":"<p>When the function only needs AWS services. Endpoints keep traffic private and avoid NAT hourly and data processing charges. Gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB are free. Interface endpoints cost per hour per AZ and per GB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0181","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"A VPC-attached Lambda function times out reading from DynamoDB. What are two fixes?","a":"<p>Add a DynamoDB gateway endpoint to the subnet route tables, or route through a NAT gateway. Without either, the DynamoDB endpoint is unreachable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0182","card":"q-lambda-035-internet-access-and-vpc-endpoints","sec":"lambda-s036","q":"How can you restrict what a VPC endpoint is used for?","a":"<p>Attach an endpoint policy that allows only particular actions and resources, for example one S3 bucket. It limits data exfiltration through the endpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0183","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"What is the default regional concurrency limit in a new account?","a":"<p>1,000 concurrent executions per region (a soft limit, raised through a Service Quotas request), shared across all functions in that region and account.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0184","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"Reserved Concurrency vs Provisioned Concurrency?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Reserved Concurrency</strong> carves out a guaranteed slice of the account limit for one function and also caps that function at that number. It manages <em>how much</em> concurrency a function can use, at no extra cost.</li>\n  <li><strong>Provisioned Concurrency</strong> pre-warms N environments so invocations skip cold starts. It manages <em>latency and readiness</em>, and you pay for the pre-warmed capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name f --reserved-concurrent-executions 100</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0185","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"What happens when a function exceeds its concurrency limit?","a":"<p>Lambda throttles: it rejects extra concurrent invocations with a <code>429 TooManyRequestsException</code> (<code>ThrottleReason</code>). What happens next depends on the invocation type.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0186","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"How do sync calls react to throttling vs async calls?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Synchronous</strong>: the caller immediately gets a <code>429</code> and must handle or retry it (API Gateway surfaces a 5xx or 429 to the client).</li>\n  <li><strong>Asynchronous</strong>: Lambda keeps the event in its internal queue and retries for up to about 6 hours with backoff. If it never succeeds, the event goes to the DLQ or on-failure destination. Async throttling is largely absorbed, while sync throttling is felt by the caller.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0187","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"What status code does a throttled synchronous API Gateway request return?","a":"<p>Lambda returns <code>429 TooManyRequestsException</code>. Depending on integration config, API Gateway may show the client a <code>429</code>, or a <code>502</code>/<code>500</code> if not mapped. The Lambda-level reason is a throttle.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0188","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"What does setting a Lambda function's reserved concurrency to 0 do, and when is it used?","a":"<p>It blocks all invocations, so every call is throttled. It works as an emergency stop for a runaway or compromised function without deleting it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0189","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"How does reserved concurrency for one function affect other functions in the account?","a":"<p>The reserved amount is taken from the shared regional pool, so other functions have less unreserved capacity. Lambda keeps at least 100 unreserved concurrency for functions without reservation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0190","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"Which CloudWatch metrics distinguish function errors from throttling in Lambda?","a":"<p><code>Errors</code> counts invocations that failed in code, and <code>Throttles</code> counts invocations Lambda rejected for lack of concurrency. Throttled invocations never ran, so they do not appear as errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0191","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"How would you alert early that a Lambda function is about to hit its concurrency limit?","a":"<p>Create an alarm on <code>ConcurrentExecutions</code> at a percentage of the account or reserved limit and an alarm on <code>Throttles</code> greater than zero.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0192","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"A Lambda function behind API Gateway returns 429 under load. How do you investigate and fix it?","a":"<p>Look at the <code>Throttles</code> metric and concurrency usage. Raise the account quota, adjust reserved concurrency, shorten duration to lower concurrency, or smooth spikes with a queue. Also check API Gateway's own throttling settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0193","card":"q-lambda-036-concurrency-limits-and-throttling","sec":"lambda-s037","q":"In AWS Lambda, what happens to a throttled invocation for synchronous, asynchronous and SQS-triggered calls, and how do you raise the account limit?","a":"<p>Synchronous: the caller gets a 429 <code>TooManyRequestsException</code> and must retry. Asynchronous: Lambda queues the event and retries for up to 6 hours by default. SQS event source: the message returns to the queue after the visibility timeout and is retried until it reaches the DLQ via <code>maxReceiveCount</code>. The account concurrency quota is a soft limit raised through Service Quotas (default 1,000 per Region).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0194","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"How is total concurrency computed?","a":"<p>Concurrency = requests per second x average duration in seconds. For example 100 req/s x 0.2 s = 20 concurrent executions. This (Little's Law) tells you how much to reserve or provision and whether you will approach the limit.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0195","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"What is burst concurrency, and how does scaling work beyond it?","a":"<p>On a sudden spike Lambda provides an initial burst of environments instantly, then scales at a steady rate (for example +500 environments per function per minute in supported regions) until demand is met or the limit is reached. During ramp-up, requests beyond available concurrency are throttled (sync) or queued (async).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0196","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"How do you monitor concurrency across a region?","a":"<p>Watch the CloudWatch <code>ConcurrentExecutions</code> metric (account-wide and per function) and <code>UnreservedConcurrentExecutions</code>. Alarm as you approach the account limit, and use <code>ClaimedAccountConcurrency</code> plus the Lambda console and Service Quotas to track headroom.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0197","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"A Lambda function receives 500 requests per second at 400 ms each. How many concurrent executions does it need, and what if duration rises to 2 s?","a":"<p>500 x 0.4 = 200 concurrent executions. At 2 s it is 500 x 2 = 1,000, which reaches the default account limit. A slow downstream service can therefore cause throttling even when traffic is unchanged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0198","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"Why does reducing function duration also reduce the concurrency a Lambda workload needs?","a":"<p>Concurrency is arrival rate times duration, so faster functions free their environments sooner and the same traffic needs fewer of them. That protects the account quota and downstream systems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0199","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"Your Lambda API faces a sudden spike from 0 to several thousand requests per second and clients see 429 errors. What do you do?","a":"<p>Scaling happens at a bounded rate after an initial burst, so a very sharp spike can outrun it. Use Provisioned Concurrency before expected events, put a queue in front to absorb spikes, and request quota increases where appropriate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0200","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"How would you size Provisioned Concurrency for a function?","a":"<p>Estimate peak concurrency (requests per second x duration), then provision around the steady portion and let the rest scale on demand. Use scheduled or target tracking scaling to follow daily patterns and avoid paying for idle capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0201","card":"q-lambda-037-concurrency-math-and-burst-scaling","sec":"lambda-s038","q":"How fast does AWS Lambda scale out when traffic spikes?","a":"<p>Since the November 2023 scaling change, each function can add up to 1,000 concurrent executions every 10 seconds, until it reaches the account or reserved concurrency limit. Older documentation described a Region-specific initial burst (500 to 3,000) plus 500 per minute. A spike steeper than this rate is throttled, so use Provisioned Concurrency or a queue for sharp peaks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0202","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"How does Reserved Concurrency protect downstream resources such as databases?","a":"<p>Capping a function's maximum concurrency limits how many simultaneous connections it can open to a fragile downstream, such as an RDS instance with few connections. This stops Lambda's rapid scaling from overwhelming the database. Pair it with RDS Proxy for pooling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0203","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"What if one function monopolizes all unreserved concurrency?","a":"<p>Other functions get throttled because they compete for the same shared pool. Set <strong>reserved concurrency</strong> on high-traffic functions (to cap them) and reserve concurrency for critical functions so they always have headroom. For example, with a 1,000 pool: a critical function reserved 200 (guaranteed and capped), a batch function reserved 100, and the remaining 700 unreserved are shared by everything else.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0204","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"How do you prevent runaway cost from recursive loops?","a":"<p>Lambda has built-in <strong>recursive loop detection</strong> (for example Lambda to SQS/SNS to the same Lambda) that stops the loop and notifies you. Also set <strong>reserved concurrency</strong> to cap the blast radius, add CloudWatch billing and concurrency alarms, avoid a function writing to its own trigger source, and use idempotency plus circuit breakers.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Cap a function so a runaway loop can&#x27;t consume the whole account limit\naws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name f --reserved-concurrent-executions 10</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0205","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"A burst of Lambda invocations overwhelms a database connection limit. What are the options?","a":"<p>Cap concurrency (reserved concurrency or <code>MaximumConcurrency</code>), buffer with a queue, and pool connections with a proxy. Reuse connections across warm invocations, and keep per-environment connection use small.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0206","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"How would you prevent a recursive Lambda loop from running up a large bill?","a":"<p>Never write back to the trigger source without a guard, add reserved concurrency as a cap, use Lambda's recursive loop detection, set billing and concurrency alarms, and keep the logic idempotent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0207","card":"q-lambda-038-protecting-downstream-systems-and-costs","sec":"lambda-s039","q":"What is a good way to protect a fragile third-party API called from Lambda?","a":"<p>Put a queue between producers and the function, limit concurrency to the API's rate, add retries with backoff and jitter, and use a circuit breaker so failures do not pile up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0208","card":"q-lambda-039-soft-limits-vs-hard-limits","sec":"lambda-s040","q":"What are soft limits vs hard limits?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Soft limits (quotas)</strong>: adjustable through Service Quotas, for example the default 1,000 concurrency and function or layer storage.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hard limits</strong>: fixed by the platform, for example the 15-min timeout, 6 MB sync and 256 KB async payload, 10 GB memory, 10 GB <code>/tmp</code>, 250 MB unzipped zip and 512 KB total env var size. Design around hard limits and request increases for soft ones.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0209","card":"q-lambda-039-soft-limits-vs-hard-limits","sec":"lambda-s040","q":"In AWS Lambda, how would you handle a requirement that exceeds a hard limit such as the 15-minute timeout?","a":"<p>Hard limits cannot be raised, so change the design. Break the work into steps with Step Functions, move it to Fargate or AWS Batch, or pass large payloads through S3 instead of the invocation payload.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0210","card":"q-lambda-039-soft-limits-vs-hard-limits","sec":"lambda-s040","q":"What is the practical difference between a soft and a hard limit when planning a Lambda workload?","a":"<p>Soft limits, such as account concurrency, can be raised through Service Quotas ahead of launch. Hard limits are fixed, so the architecture must fit within them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0211","card":"q-lambda-040-stateless-design-and-where-to-keep-state","sec":"lambda-s041","q":"Why is Lambda stateless, and where should application state live?","a":"<p>Each execution environment is ephemeral and any invocation may land on a fresh one, so nothing in memory or <code>/tmp</code> is guaranteed to persist. Store state externally: <strong>DynamoDB</strong> (key/value, sessions, counters, idempotency), <strong>S3</strong> (objects and files), <strong>RDS/Aurora</strong> (relational) and <strong>ElastiCache</strong> (fast shared cache). Warm-environment caching is a performance optimization, not a correctness guarantee.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Persistence</th><th>Scope</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><code>/tmp</code> (512 MB to 10 GB)</td><td>ephemeral</td><td>single environment</td><td>scratch files during processing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>EFS</td><td>durable</td><td>shared, many functions</td><td>large models, shared file system, POSIX (needs VPC)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>S3</td><td>durable</td><td>global</td><td>inputs and outputs, blobs, artifacts</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>DynamoDB</td><td>durable</td><td>global</td><td>sessions, counters, key/value, idempotency</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>RDS/Aurora + RDS Proxy</td><td>durable</td><td>shared</td><td>relational data (proxy pools connections)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>ElastiCache</td><td>in-memory</td><td>shared</td><td>hot cache, fast shared state</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0212","card":"q-lambda-040-stateless-design-and-where-to-keep-state","sec":"lambda-s041","q":"In a Lambda function, why use an atomic update such as DynamoDB <code>ADD</code> for a counter instead of reading the value and writing it back?","a":"<p>With read-then-write, two concurrent invocations can read the same value and overwrite each other's update. <code>ADD</code> increments in the database in a single atomic operation, so no update is lost.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ddb.update_item(\n    TableName=&quot;Counters&quot;, Key={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: &quot;hits&quot;}},\n    UpdateExpression=&quot;ADD #c :one&quot;,\n    ...\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0213","card":"q-lambda-040-stateless-design-and-where-to-keep-state","sec":"lambda-s041","q":"Where would you keep user session data for a Lambda-backed web application?","a":"<p>In an external store. DynamoDB (with a TTL attribute) is the common choice for serverless, ElastiCache fits very low-latency sessions, and a signed token such as a JWT keeps the session on the client. Never rely on environment memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0214","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"How do you attach an EFS volume to a function?","a":"<p>Put the function in the same VPC as the EFS mount target, create an <strong>EFS Access Point</strong>, and set the function's <code>FileSystemConfig</code> to mount it at a local path (for example <code>/mnt/data</code>). The execution role needs EFS permissions. The function then reads and writes EFS like a shared, durable local file system.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    VpcConfig: { SubnetIds: [...], SecurityGroupIds: [...] }\n    FileSystemConfigs:\n      - Arn: !GetAtt AccessPoint.Arn\n        LocalMountPath: /mnt/data       # must start with /mnt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0215","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"When use <code>/tmp</code>, EFS or S3?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>/tmp</code></strong> (512 MB to 10 GB): fast, local, ephemeral scratch space for one invocation or warm environment.</li>\n  <li><strong>EFS</strong>: a shared, durable, low-latency POSIX file system across many concurrent functions. Use for large models, shared state, or when you need a file system. Requires VPC.</li>\n  <li><strong>S3</strong>: durable, massively scalable object storage for inputs, outputs, artifacts and large blobs accessed over the API (no VPC needed).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0216","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"In AWS Lambda, how do you choose between <code>/tmp</code>, EFS and S3 for data a function needs?","a":"<p>Pick by lifetime and sharing. <code>/tmp</code> is fast local scratch space for one execution environment and is lost when the environment goes away. EFS is a durable, shared POSIX file system that many concurrent functions can mount. S3 is durable object storage reached over the API, best for inputs, outputs and large blobs. Rule of thumb: S3 by default, EFS when you need a real shared file system, <code>/tmp</code> only for temporary work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0217","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is the trade-off of mounting EFS compared with reading files from S3?","a":"<p>EFS gives file-system semantics and low latency for shared files such as large ML models or libraries, and the function does not have to download them each cold start. The cost: the function must be attached to a VPC with a mount target, EFS is more expensive per GB than S3, and a bad network or security group setup breaks cold starts. S3 needs no VPC and is cheaper, but you copy the object to <code>/tmp</code> or stream it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0218","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"In this SAM snippet, what does <code>LocalMountPath</code> do and what restriction applies to it?","a":"<p><code>LocalMountPath</code> is the directory inside the execution environment where the EFS access point appears, so the code reads and writes <code>/mnt/data/...</code> like a local folder. The path must start with <code>/mnt</code>. The function also needs <code>VpcConfig</code> in the same VPC as the mount target, and an execution role allowed to use EFS.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    FileSystemConfigs:\n      - Arn: !GetAtt AccessPoint.Arn\n        LocalMountPath: /mnt/data</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0219","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"In AWS Lambda with EFS, why is an EFS access point used instead of mounting the file system root?","a":"<p>An access point enforces a specific POSIX user and group and a root directory for the function. That limits what the function can see and write, so different functions can share one file system without access to each other's folders. It is least privilege at the file-system level.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0220","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"A Lambda function writes a large file to <code>/tmp</code> during one invocation. What can you rely on in the next invocation?","a":"<p>Nothing is guaranteed. If the next request lands on the same warm environment, the file is still there. If it lands on a new environment, it is not. Use <code>/tmp</code> as a cache you can rebuild, and keep anything that must survive in S3, EFS or a database. Also delete large temp files, because leftover files in a reused environment count against the <code>/tmp</code> size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0221","card":"q-lambda-041-efs-vs-tmp-vs-s3","sec":"lambda-s042","q":"Your Lambda function needs more than 512 MB of scratch space. What are the options?","a":"<p>Raise the function's ephemeral storage setting (<code>/tmp</code>) up to 10,240 MB, which costs extra per GB-second beyond the default 512 MB. If the data must be shared or kept, use EFS or S3. If the data can be streamed, process it from S3 as a stream without writing it to disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0222","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"How does Step Functions orchestrate stateful workflows with Lambda?","a":"<p>Step Functions is a managed state machine that coordinates several Lambdas (and other services) with sequencing, branching (<code>Choice</code>), parallelism (<code>Map</code>/<code>Parallel</code>), retries, error handling and wait states. The workflow state is external, so each Lambda stays stateless and short. It suits long-running, multi-step processes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;ResizeImage&quot;: {\n    &quot;Type&quot;: &quot;Task&quot;,\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:...:function:resize&quot;,\n    &quot;Retry&quot;: [{ &quot;ErrorEquals&quot;: [&quot;States.TaskFailed&quot;], &quot;MaxAttempts&quot;: 2 }],\n    &quot;Next&quot;: &quot;Notify&quot; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0223","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"What is the max payload Step Functions passes between Lambda tasks?","a":"<p>256 KB per state transition. For larger data pass a <strong>reference</strong> (for example an S3 key) and have the Lambdas read and write the real payload in S3.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0224","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"How do you manage DB connection pooling with RDS Proxy and Lambda?","a":"<p>Lambda's rapid scaling can exhaust database connections. <strong>RDS Proxy</strong> sits between Lambda and RDS or Aurora, keeps a warm pool and multiplexes many Lambda connections onto fewer DB connections. Lambda connects to the proxy endpoint (IAM auth supported), which smooths spikes and improves failover. Pair it with reserved concurrency.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Connect to RDS through RDS Proxy (endpoint = proxy, not the DB directly)\nimport os, pymysql\nconn = pymysql.connect(host=os.environ[&quot;PROXY_ENDPOINT&quot;],  # created outside handler\n                       user=os.environ[&quot;DB_USER&quot;], db=&quot;app&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    with conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.execute(&quot;SELECT 1&quot;)\n        return cur.fetchone()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0225","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In AWS, why use Step Functions to coordinate several Lambda functions instead of one Lambda calling the next?","a":"<p>Step Functions keeps the workflow state outside the functions, so each Lambda stays short and stateless. It gives you sequencing, <code>Choice</code> branching, <code>Parallel</code> and <code>Map</code>, retries with backoff, error catching and wait states without writing that code. Chained direct invocations pay for waiting, lose context when one step fails, and are hard to trace. The cost is an extra service and a charge per state transition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0226","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In a Step Functions state machine, what does this <code>Retry</code> block on a Lambda <code>Task</code> do?","a":"<p>If the task fails with an error matching <code>ErrorEquals</code>, Step Functions runs it again, up to <code>MaxAttempts</code> retries, before treating it as failed. Only after retries are exhausted does a <code>Catch</code> or the state machine failure happen. <code>Next</code> names the state that runs after success. Pair retries with idempotent Lambda code, because a retried task may run twice.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;ResizeImage&quot;: {\n    &quot;Type&quot;: &quot;Task&quot;,\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:...:function:resize&quot;,\n    &quot;Retry&quot;: [{ &quot;ErrorEquals&quot;: [&quot;States.TaskFailed&quot;], &quot;MaxAttempts&quot;: 2 }],\n    &quot;Next&quot;: &quot;Notify&quot; } }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0227","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In AWS Step Functions, how do you pass a large file between Lambda tasks when the payload limit is 256 KB?","a":"<p>Store the data in S3 (or another store) and pass only a reference such as the bucket and key through the state. Each Lambda reads and writes the real data in S3. The same pattern applies to any large intermediate result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0228","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In Step Functions, which state types let you do work for many items in parallel, and how do they differ?","a":"<p><code>Map</code> runs the same steps for each item of an input array. <code>Parallel</code> runs several different branches at the same time on the same input. Both wait for all branches and return an array of results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0229","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In AWS Step Functions, when would you use the <code>WaitForTaskToken</code> pattern with Lambda?","a":"<p>When a step has to wait for something outside Step Functions, such as a human approval or a slow third-party job. The task passes a token to Lambda or another service and pauses without paying for compute. When the work finishes, the caller sends the token back with <code>SendTaskSuccess</code> or <code>SendTaskFailure</code> and the workflow resumes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0230","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In AWS Lambda, what problem does RDS Proxy solve for functions that use a relational database?","a":"<p>Each concurrent Lambda environment opens its own database connection, so a scale-out can exhaust the connection limit of RDS or Aurora. RDS Proxy keeps a pool of warm connections and multiplexes many Lambda connections onto fewer database connections. It also speeds up failover and supports IAM authentication and Secrets Manager credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0231","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In this Python Lambda code, why is the database connection created outside the handler, and what is still a risk?","a":"<p>Code outside the handler runs once per execution environment, so warm invocations reuse the connection instead of reconnecting each time. The risk is a stale connection: the database or proxy can close an idle connection while the environment is frozen, and the next call fails. Add a reconnect or a health check on error. Also, if the connection is opened at init and the database is down, the cold start fails.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import os, pymysql\nconn = pymysql.connect(host=os.environ[&quot;PROXY_ENDPOINT&quot;],\n                       user=os.environ[&quot;DB_USER&quot;], db=&quot;app&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    with conn.cursor() as cur:\n        cur.execute(&quot;SELECT 1&quot;)\n        return cur.fetchone()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0232","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"In AWS Lambda, why is RDS Proxy often paired with reserved concurrency?","a":"<p>The proxy smooths connection spikes, but it has its own connection limit. Reserved concurrency caps how many environments, and so how many client connections, the function can ever open, which protects the proxy and the database. Together they put a ceiling on the load a traffic spike can create.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0233","card":"q-lambda-042-step-functions-and-rds-proxy","sec":"lambda-s043","q":"When is RDS Proxy not needed for a Lambda function that talks to a database?","a":"<p>When the function is the only light client and concurrency is low, when the data store is DynamoDB (an HTTP API with no connection pool), or when you use the RDS Data API. Proxy costs money and requires VPC placement, so skip it unless connection exhaustion or failover time is a real problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0234","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"How does Lambda integrate with DynamoDB Streams for change data capture?","a":"<p>Enable a stream on the table. An <strong>event source mapping</strong> polls it and invokes Lambda with ordered batches of change records (<code>INSERT</code>/<code>MODIFY</code>/<code>REMOVE</code>, with old and new images). Use it for CDC, replication, aggregations, notifications and materialized views.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    for r in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        if r[&quot;eventName&quot;] == &quot;INSERT&quot;:\n            new_item = r[&quot;dynamodb&quot;][&quot;NewImage&quot;]\n            process(new_item)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0235","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"What is Lambda@Edge, and how does it differ from standard Lambda?","a":"<p>Lambda@Edge runs functions at CloudFront edge locations, close to users, on CloudFront events (viewer or origin request and response). It customizes or inspects HTTP at the edge (redirects, header changes, A/B tests, auth). Compared with standard Lambda it is replicated globally to the edge, has tighter limits (size, timeout, no VPC or env vars) and supports only Node.js and Python.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0236","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"What are CloudFront Functions, and how do they compare with Lambda@Edge?","a":"<p>CloudFront Functions are very lightweight JavaScript functions that run at the edge on viewer request and response, with sub-millisecond startup, massive scale and very low cost, but tight constraints (very short runtime, tiny memory, no network or file access). Use them for simple high-volume tasks (header rewrites, redirects, token checks). Use <strong>Lambda@Edge</strong> when you need more compute, longer execution, network calls or origin-event access.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0237","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS, how does Lambda consume a DynamoDB stream, and who calls the function?","a":"<p>You enable a stream on the table and create an event source mapping. The Lambda service polls the stream and invokes the function with batches of change records, in order per item. The function does not poll; the mapping does, so the function's execution role needs permission to read the stream (<code>dynamodb:GetRecords</code>, <code>GetShardIterator</code>, <code>DescribeStream</code>, <code>ListStreams</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0238","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS DynamoDB Streams, what are the four stream view types, and how do you choose?","a":"<p><code>KEYS_ONLY</code> (only the key attributes), <code>NEW_IMAGE</code> (the item after the change), <code>OLD_IMAGE</code> (the item before), and <code>NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES</code> (both). Choose the smallest one that works. You need both images to compute a diff or audit change, only <code>NEW_IMAGE</code> to replicate or index, and <code>KEYS_ONLY</code> if the function re-reads the item itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0239","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In this Lambda handler for a DynamoDB stream, what is the missing handling for deletes, and what changes?","a":"<p>Only <code>INSERT</code> records are handled, so <code>MODIFY</code> and <code>REMOVE</code> are silently ignored. A <code>REMOVE</code> record has no <code>NewImage</code>; it carries <code>OldImage</code> (if the view type includes it) and <code>Keys</code>. Handle each <code>eventName</code> explicitly, or filter at the event source mapping so Lambda is only invoked for the events you care about.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    for r in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        if r[&quot;eventName&quot;] == &quot;INSERT&quot;:\n            new_item = r[&quot;dynamodb&quot;][&quot;NewImage&quot;]\n            process(new_item)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0240","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS Lambda, how can you avoid invoking a function for stream records it will ignore?","a":"<p>Add event filtering (filter criteria) to the event source mapping. Lambda drops non-matching records before invoking the function, so you pay for fewer invocations. Filtering works for DynamoDB, Kinesis and SQS mappings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0241","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"What are typical use cases for a Lambda function triggered by DynamoDB Streams, and what ordering guarantee do you get?","a":"<p>Change data capture, replication to another table or region, search index updates, aggregates and materialised views, audit trails and notifications. Records for one item arrive in the order they changed, because they stay in the same shard. There is no ordering across different items. Stream records are kept for 24 hours.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0242","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"What happens to a DynamoDB stream consumer in Lambda when one record keeps failing, and how do you contain it?","a":"<p>By default Lambda retries the batch until the record succeeds or expires from the stream (24 hours), and the shard is blocked behind it. Contain it by limiting retry attempts and record age, enabling <code>BisectBatchOnFunctionError</code>, reporting partial failures with <code>ReportBatchItemFailures</code>, and sending failed batch metadata to an on-failure destination such as SQS or SNS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0243","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS, how does Lambda@Edge differ from a regular Lambda function?","a":"<p>Lambda@Edge runs your code at CloudFront edge locations in response to CloudFront events (viewer request, origin request, origin response, viewer response). You author it in <code>us-east-1</code> and CloudFront replicates it. Compared with a regular function it has tighter limits, supports a limited set of runtimes (Node.js and Python), and cannot use VPC access, environment variables or layers. Use it to customise HTTP traffic close to users.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0244","card":"q-lambda-043-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"A CloudFront distribution needs to redirect requests and rewrite headers for millions of requests per second at very low cost. Which edge option do you pick and why?","a":"<p>Use CloudFront Functions. They are lightweight JavaScript functions that start in under a millisecond and are much cheaper than Lambda@Edge, suited to header rewrites, redirects, URL normalisation and token checks. Choose Lambda@Edge only when you need more time or memory, network calls, or access to the request body.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0245","card":"q-lambda-044-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"How do Lambda Extensions work, and what are internal vs external extensions?","a":"<p>Extensions plug tools (observability, security, config) into the Lambda lifecycle through the Extensions API. <strong>Internal extensions</strong> run in-process inside the runtime (wrapper scripts, language agents). <strong>External extensions</strong> run as separate processes alongside the function, can start during Init and keep running through Invoke and Shutdown. They suit shipping logs and metrics and caching secrets without blocking the handler.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0246","card":"q-lambda-044-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are the limits of CloudFront Functions compared with Lambda@Edge?","a":"<p>CloudFront Functions run only on viewer request and viewer response events, in a restricted JavaScript runtime with a very short execution time and tiny memory, and they have no network or file-system access and cannot read the request body. Lambda@Edge also handles origin request and origin response events, runs longer, can call other services over the network and can read the body, at higher cost and with higher latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0247","card":"q-lambda-044-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is the difference between an internal and an external extension?","a":"<p>An internal extension runs inside the runtime process and is loaded through a language agent or wrapper script, so it shares the runtime's lifecycle. An external extension is a separate process that starts during Init, keeps running during Invoke, and gets a chance to finish during Shutdown. External extensions can be written in any language because they are separate executables in a layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0248","card":"q-lambda-044-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS Lambda, how does an external extension affect cost and latency?","a":"<p>Extension time is part of the invocation. Its Init work extends cold start. Because the invocation is not finished until the extension signals that it is done, slow work in an extension raises the billed duration. Keep extensions light, and use them for shipping logs and metrics or caching secrets instead of doing heavy work on the request path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0249","card":"q-lambda-044-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"In AWS Lambda, how are extensions packaged and attached to a function?","a":"<p>Extensions are usually distributed as layers that put the executable in the <code>/opt/extensions</code> directory. You attach the layer to the function (or include the files in a container image) and Lambda starts them on its own. Observability vendors ship their agents this way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0250","card":"q-lambda-044-dynamodb-streams-edge-functions-and-exte","sec":"lambda-s044","q":"Why would a team use a Lambda extension for secrets or configuration instead of calling Secrets Manager in the handler?","a":"<p>An extension can fetch and cache secrets or parameters outside the handler and expose them over a local HTTP endpoint. That keeps the handler fast, avoids repeated API calls and throttling, and lets the cache refresh with a TTL. AWS provides a Parameters and Secrets extension layer for this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0251","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"What are Lambda Layers, and how do they manage shared code?","a":"<p>A Layer is a <code>.zip</code> archive of libraries, custom runtimes or shared code that you attach to functions. It is extracted into <code>/opt</code> at runtime. Layers let several functions share dependencies without bundling them into each package: smaller deployment packages, faster deploys and central versioning.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyLayer:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::LayerVersion\n  Properties:\n    ContentUri: dependencies/         # e.g. python/lib/... structure\n    CompatibleRuntimes: [python3.12]\nMyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    Layers: [!Ref MyLayer]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0252","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"What are the limits on Layers?","a":"<p>Up to <strong>5 layers</strong> per function. The <strong>combined unzipped size</strong> of function code and all layers must be at most <strong>250 MB</strong>. Layers count toward the same limit, so they save package size but not the total footprint.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0253","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"In AWS Lambda, why would you move shared dependencies into a layer?","a":"<p>A layer is a zip that is extracted to <code>/opt</code> and shared by many functions. It keeps each function's deployment package small, lets you version the shared code in one place, and speeds up deploys of the function code alone. It does not make the function smaller at runtime: layers count toward the unzipped size limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0254","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are the size and count limits for layers?","a":"<p>A function can use up to 5 layers, and the total unzipped size of the function code plus all its layers must be at most 250 MB. These are hard limits that cannot be raised. For bigger dependencies use a container image (up to 10 GB) or EFS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0255","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"In this SAM template, what do <code>ContentUri</code> and <code>CompatibleRuntimes</code> do on the layer?","a":"<p><code>ContentUri</code> is the local folder SAM zips and uploads as the layer content. <code>CompatibleRuntimes</code> records which runtimes the layer is meant for, which the console and tooling use to filter layers when you attach one. <code>Layers</code> on the function lists the layer versions to attach, and <code>!Ref MyLayer</code> resolves to the layer version ARN.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyLayer:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::LayerVersion\n  Properties:\n    ContentUri: dependencies/\n    CompatibleRuntimes: [python3.12]\nMyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    Layers: [!Ref MyLayer]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0256","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"In AWS Lambda, how must a Python layer's zip be structured so that imports work?","a":"<p>Put the packages under a <code>python/</code> folder at the top of the zip (for example <code>python/lib/python3.12/site-packages/...</code>). Lambda extracts the layer into <code>/opt</code>, and <code>/opt/python</code> is on the Python path. Other runtimes use their own folders, such as <code>nodejs/node_modules</code> for Node.js.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0257","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"In AWS Lambda, what happens to a function when you publish a new version of a layer?","a":"<p>Nothing automatically. Layer versions are immutable and a function points to a specific layer version ARN. You have to update the function configuration to use the new version. This makes rollouts and rollbacks explicit, but it also means a security fix in a layer needs every consumer function to be updated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0258","card":"q-lambda-045-layers","sec":"lambda-s045","q":"What are the drawbacks of layers, and when would you not use them?","a":"<p>Layers hide dependencies from the function package, so local testing and reproducible builds are harder. They are limited to 5 per function and share the 250 MB unzipped limit, and they cannot be used with container-image functions. Sharing a layer across teams can break functions when a new layer version changes behaviour. For large or complex dependencies, a container image is simpler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0259","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"In AWS Lambda, which metrics appear in CloudWatch without any code change, and what does each tell you?","a":"<p><code>Invocations</code> (traffic), <code>Errors</code> (failed invocations), <code>Duration</code> (execution time, for tuning memory and timeout), <code>Throttles</code> (rejected for lack of concurrency) and <code>ConcurrentExecutions</code> (how many environments run at once). Stream and async functions also publish <code>IteratorAge</code> and async event metrics. They are published automatically by the service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0260","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"In AWS Lambda, how do logs reach CloudWatch Logs, and how are they organised?","a":"<p>Anything the function writes to stdout or stderr (<code>print</code>, <code>console.log</code>, a logger) goes to CloudWatch Logs through the execution role. Each function gets a log group named <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code>, each execution environment writes to its own log stream inside that group, and each line is a log event. Lambda also writes <code>START</code>, <code>END</code> and <code>REPORT</code> lines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0261","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"In AWS Lambda, how do you control the cost of CloudWatch Logs for a function?","a":"<p>Set a retention period on the log group, because the default is to keep logs forever. Log sparingly at a sensible level, avoid dumping large payloads on every call, and consider sampling debug logs. Logging cost often exceeds compute cost for chatty functions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0262","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"In the <code>REPORT</code> line that Lambda writes, what is the difference between <code>Duration</code> and <code>Billed Duration</code>?","a":"<p><code>Duration</code> is the time the handler ran. <code>Billed Duration</code> is that rounded up to the next millisecond. Since August 1, 2025, the init time of on-demand zip-packaged functions on managed runtimes is also included in <code>Billed Duration</code>. Before that date it was not billed for those functions.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>REPORT Duration: 123.45 ms Billed Duration: 124 ms Memory Size: 128 MB Max Memory Used: 50 MB Init Duration: 100.00 ms</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0263","card":"q-lambda-046-cloudwatch-metrics-and-logs-for-lambda","sec":"lambda-s046","q":"In AWS Lambda, which CloudWatch alarms would you create for a production function?","a":"<p>Alarms on <code>Errors</code> (or an error rate using metric math with <code>Invocations</code>), <code>Throttles</code>, and <code>Duration</code> near the timeout (for example p99). For queue and stream consumers add the queue age or <code>IteratorAge</code>. Send alarms to SNS so someone is paged, and use them to gate canary deployments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0264","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"Where are Lambda execution logs stored by default?","a":"<p>In <strong>Amazon CloudWatch Logs</strong>, in a log group named <code>/aws/lambda/&lt;function-name&gt;</code>, split into log streams per execution environment. Anything printed (<code>console.log</code>, <code>print</code>, stdout and stderr) plus the automatic <code>START</code>/<code>END</code>/<code>REPORT</code> lines land there. The execution role needs <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code>, <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0265","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"Which CloudWatch metrics does Lambda publish automatically?","a":"<p><code>Invocations</code>, <code>Errors</code>, <code>Duration</code>, <code>Throttles</code>, <code>ConcurrentExecutions</code>, <code>DeadLetterErrors</code>, <code>IteratorAge</code> (streams), <code>ProvisionedConcurrency*</code> metrics and <code>Async*</code> event metrics. From them you derive error rate, latency percentiles and concurrency headroom.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0266","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"What is AWS X-Ray, and how do you trace a function?","a":"<p>X-Ray is a distributed tracing service. Enable <strong>Active Tracing</strong> on the function. Lambda emits a trace segment per invocation, and the X-Ray SDK auto-instruments downstream calls (DynamoDB, HTTP and so on) as subsegments. You see the end-to-end request path and latency bottlenecks and errors on a service map.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from aws_xray_sdk.core import xray_recorder, patch_all\npatch_all()   # auto-trace boto3 / requests calls\n\n@xray_recorder.capture(&quot;business_logic&quot;)\ndef do_work(event):\n    ...</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0267","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"In AWS, what problem does X-Ray solve that CloudWatch logs and metrics do not?","a":"<p>X-Ray traces one request across services. Logs and metrics tell you that something is slow or failing, while a trace shows where the time went: which downstream call, DynamoDB query or HTTP request inside the request path. It draws a service map and lets you find latency bottlenecks and errors per segment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0268","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"How do you turn on X-Ray tracing for a Lambda function, and what extra permission is involved?","a":"<p>Enable Active Tracing on the function (console, CLI <code>--tracing-config Mode=Active</code>, or SAM <code>Tracing: Active</code>). The execution role needs <code>xray:PutTraceSegments</code> and <code>xray:PutTelemetryRecords</code>, which the managed <code>AWSXRayDaemonWriteAccess</code> policy covers. Lambda then records a segment for each invocation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0269","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"In this Python code, what does <code>patch_all()</code> do and what does <code>@xray_recorder.capture</code> add?","a":"<p><code>patch_all()</code> instruments supported libraries such as <code>boto3</code> and <code>requests</code>, so every downstream call appears as a subsegment with its timing. The decorator wraps your own function in a custom subsegment named <code>business_logic</code>, so you can see how long your code took compared with calls it makes. Call <code>patch_all()</code> before creating clients.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from aws_xray_sdk.core import xray_recorder, patch_all\npatch_all()\n\n@xray_recorder.capture(&quot;business_logic&quot;)\ndef do_work(event):\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0270","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"In AWS Lambda, what does the <code>IteratorAge</code> metric show and when do you alarm on it?","a":"<p>It is the age of the last record in a batch read from a Kinesis or DynamoDB stream when the function received it. A rising value means the consumer is falling behind or stuck on a failing record. Alarm on it for stream-based functions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0271","card":"q-lambda-047-logs-metrics-and-x-ray","sec":"lambda-s047","q":"In AWS Lambda, what does the <code>DeadLetterErrors</code> metric tell you?","a":"<p>It counts failures when Lambda tried to send an event to the configured dead letter queue or topic and could not, for example because the role lacks permission. It is a sign that failed events are being lost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0272","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"How do you compute total billable duration from <code>REPORT</code> logs?","a":"<p>Each <code>REPORT</code> line shows <code>Billed Duration</code> (ms, rounded up to the nearest 1 ms) and <code>Memory Size</code>. Cost is about the sum of (Billed Duration in seconds x Memory in GB) x price per GB-second, plus the per-request cost. Sum <code>Billed Duration</code> across invocations (or use CloudWatch <code>Duration</code> x count) to estimate billable time.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>REPORT RequestId: ...  Duration: 123.45 ms  Billed Duration: 124 ms  Memory Size: 128 MB  Max Memory Used: 50 MB</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0273","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"How do you write structured JSON logs for aggregators?","a":"<p>Emit single-line JSON so CloudWatch Logs Insights and aggregators (Datadog, ELK) can parse fields. Set the function's log format to <strong>JSON</strong> (advanced logging controls) and/or log JSON yourself with a request id or correlation id.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json, logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(); log.setLevel(logging.INFO)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    log.info(json.dumps({\n        &quot;level&quot;: &quot;INFO&quot;, &quot;requestId&quot;: context.aws_request_id,\n        &quot;action&quot;: &quot;order.created&quot;, &quot;orderId&quot;: event.get(&quot;id&quot;)}))</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0274","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"What is the <code>context</code> object, and what does it provide?","a":"<p>Runtime information about the invocation: <code>aws_request_id</code>, <code>function_name</code>, <code>function_version</code>, <code>memory_limit_in_mb</code>, <code>log_group_name</code>/<code>log_stream_name</code>, <code>invoked_function_arn</code> and <code>get_remaining_time_in_millis()</code> (time left before timeout, useful to bail out or checkpoint).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    if context.get_remaining_time_in_millis() &lt; 2000:\n        return {&quot;status&quot;: &quot;deferred&quot;}      # not enough time left\n    return process(event)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0275","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"In AWS Lambda, how can you add up billable duration across invocations from the logs?","a":"<p>Use a CloudWatch Logs Insights query over the <code>REPORT</code> lines, summing <code>@billedDuration</code> and using <code>@memorySize</code>. For example, <code>filter @type = \"REPORT\" | stats sum(@billedDuration) as totalMs, count(*) as invocations</code>. Multiply seconds by GB of memory to get GB-seconds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0276","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"In AWS Lambda, why log JSON instead of plain text?","a":"<p>CloudWatch Logs Insights and tools like Datadog or an ELK stack can filter and aggregate JSON fields directly (for example by <code>requestId</code> or <code>orderId</code>) without regex parsing. Include a request ID or correlation ID so you can follow one request across functions. Lambda also has a JSON log format setting under advanced logging controls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0277","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"In this Python handler, what is <code>context.aws_request_id</code> used for in the log line?","a":"<p>It is the unique ID of this invocation, the same ID that appears in the <code>START</code>, <code>END</code> and <code>REPORT</code> lines. Putting it in each log line lets you filter all logs for one invocation and match them to a customer complaint or an X-Ray trace.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json, logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(); log.setLevel(logging.INFO)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    log.info(json.dumps({\n        &quot;level&quot;: &quot;INFO&quot;, &quot;requestId&quot;: context.aws_request_id,\n        &quot;action&quot;: &quot;order.created&quot;, &quot;orderId&quot;: event.get(&quot;id&quot;)}))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0278","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"In AWS Lambda, what does <code>get_remaining_time_in_millis()</code> on the context object let you do, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It returns the milliseconds left before the function times out. You can stop a long loop in time, save progress, requeue the remaining work or return a partial result instead of being killed mid-write. It is the basis of \"checkpoint and resume\" for batch work that can exceed the timeout.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    if context.get_remaining_time_in_millis() &lt; 2000:\n        return {&quot;status&quot;: &quot;deferred&quot;}\n    return process(event)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0279","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"In AWS Lambda, which other fields does the Python <code>context</code> object expose besides the request ID?","a":"<p><code>function_name</code>, <code>function_version</code>, <code>invoked_function_arn</code>, <code>memory_limit_in_mb</code>, <code>log_group_name</code> and <code>log_stream_name</code>. They are handy for logging, detecting which alias was invoked (from the ARN) and deriving limits at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0280","card":"q-lambda-048-billing-from-logs-structured-logs-and-th","sec":"lambda-s048","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are the pros and cons of Logs Insights compared with scanning log streams by hand?","a":"<p>Logs Insights queries a whole log group (or several) with filters, stats and sorting, so you can find the slowest invocations or count errors over a time range in seconds. It is billed per GB of data scanned, so narrow the time range and the log groups. Structured JSON logs make queries simpler and more accurate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0281","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"How do you unit test Lambda functions without calling AWS?","a":"<p>Keep business logic separate from the handler and test it directly. Mock AWS SDK calls with <strong>moto</strong> (Python) or <strong>aws-sdk-client-mock</strong> (JS), and pass a fake <code>event</code> and <code>context</code>. This runs fast in CI with no real AWS calls.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># test_handler.py: moto mocks DynamoDB, no real AWS\nfrom moto import mock_aws\nimport boto3, app\n\n@mock_aws\ndef test_handler():\n    boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;).create_table(...)   # in-memory mock\n    resp = app.lambda_handler({&quot;id&quot;: &quot;1&quot;}, None)\n    assert resp[&quot;statusCode&quot;] == 200</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0282","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"What local emulation tools exist (SAM CLI, LocalStack)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>AWS SAM CLI</strong>: <code>sam local invoke</code> and <code>sam local start-api</code> run the function or API in a Docker container that emulates the Lambda runtime.</li>\n  <li><strong>LocalStack</strong>: emulates many AWS services (S3, DynamoDB, SQS, Lambda) locally for integration tests.</li>\n  <li>Also <code>serverless-offline</code> and direct handler calls in unit tests.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam local invoke MyFunction -e events/event.json\nsam local start-api        # serves the API at http://localhost:3000</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0283","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"How do you handle unhandled exceptions or crashes?","a":"<p>Unhandled exceptions are logged to CloudWatch with the stack trace and increment the <code>Errors</code> metric. For sync calls the error returns to the caller. For async calls it triggers retries, then the DLQ or destination. Best practice: wrap handler logic in try/except, log structured errors, and let genuinely retryable failures throw so Lambda's retry and DLQ machinery engages.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    try:\n        return process(event)\n    except RetryableError:\n        raise                      # let Lambda retry / send to DLQ\n    except Exception as e:\n        log.exception(&quot;unrecoverable&quot;)   # stack trace to CloudWatch\n        return {&quot;statusCode&quot;: 500}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0284","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"How do CloudWatch Logs Insights queries help diagnose latency or failures?","a":"<p>Logs Insights queries log groups with a purpose-built language to filter, aggregate and sort: find the slowest invocations, count errors or extract fields from JSON logs, far faster than scrolling streams.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @timestamp, @duration, @message\n| filter @type = &quot;REPORT&quot;\n| sort @duration desc\n| limit 20</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0285","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"In AWS Lambda, how do you structure code so it can be unit tested without deploying?","a":"<p>Keep the business logic in plain functions that take ordinary arguments and call them from the handler, which stays thin. Tests call the logic directly, or call the handler with a hand-built <code>event</code> and a fake <code>context</code>. Create SDK clients outside the logic or inject them so tests can replace them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0286","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"In Python, why would you use <code>moto</code> to test a Lambda function that calls DynamoDB?","a":"<p><code>moto</code> intercepts <code>boto3</code> calls and serves them from an in-memory fake of the AWS service. The test needs no credentials, no network and no real table, and it runs fast and repeatably in CI. <code>@mock_aws</code> activates the mock for the test (in moto 5 and later; older versions used per-service decorators such as <code>mock_dynamodb</code>). Mocks do not prove IAM permissions or real service limits, so keep a few integration tests.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from moto import mock_aws\nimport boto3, app\n\n@mock_aws\ndef test_handler():\n    boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;).create_table(...)\n    resp = app.lambda_handler({&quot;id&quot;: &quot;1&quot;}, None)\n    assert resp[&quot;statusCode&quot;] == 200</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0287","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"In AWS, how do you choose between <code>moto</code>, SAM Local and LocalStack for testing Lambda?","a":"<p>Use <code>moto</code> (or <code>aws-sdk-client-mock</code> in JavaScript) for fast unit tests of code that calls the AWS SDK. Use SAM Local to run the real function package in a container that mimics the Lambda runtime and API Gateway. Use LocalStack to run many AWS services locally for integration tests. None replaces a test in a real AWS account for IAM and networking behaviour.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0288","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"In the AWS SAM CLI, what is the difference between <code>sam local invoke</code> and <code>sam local start-api</code>?","a":"<p><code>sam local invoke</code> runs one function once in a Docker container with the event file you give it, and prints the result. <code>sam local start-api</code> starts a local HTTP server (port 3000 by default) that routes requests to your functions the way API Gateway would, so you can test with curl or a browser. Both need Docker running.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam local invoke MyFunction -e events/event.json\nsam local start-api</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0289","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"In AWS Lambda, how should a handler treat exceptions it catches versus exceptions it lets propagate?","a":"<p>Re-raising lets Lambda see a function error, which counts in <code>Errors</code> and triggers retries and the dead letter queue or on-failure destination for async and stream calls. Swallowing an error and returning normally marks the invocation successful, so no retry happens. Catch and return only errors that retrying cannot fix, and log the stack trace first. Returning a 500 body like this is right for API Gateway proxy responses, but it hides the failure from alarms on <code>Errors</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    try:\n        return process(event)\n    except RetryableError:\n        raise\n    except Exception as e:\n        log.exception(&quot;unrecoverable&quot;)\n        return {&quot;statusCode&quot;: 500}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0290","card":"q-lambda-049-testing-locally-and-handling-crashes","sec":"lambda-s049","q":"In AWS Lambda, what happens to an unhandled exception for a synchronous call, an asynchronous call and an SQS-triggered call?","a":"<p>Synchronous: the error goes back to the caller and nothing retries automatically. Asynchronous: Lambda retries twice (3 attempts in total) then sends the event to the dead letter queue or on-failure destination. SQS event source mapping: the batch becomes visible again after the visibility timeout and is retried until the queue's redrive policy moves it to a dead letter queue.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0291","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"What problem does Infrastructure as Code solve compared with building resources in the console?","a":"<p>Console work is manual, error-prone and cannot be repeated exactly. IaC describes infrastructure in files, so the same definition can create identical test, staging and production environments, be reviewed in pull requests, versioned in Git, and rebuilt after a disaster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0292","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"What are the benefits of keeping infrastructure definitions in Git?","a":"<p>History of who changed what and why, code review before changes reach production, easy rollback to a known version, and automated checks and tests in CI. It also makes drift visible: the template is the source of truth, and the live account should match it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0293","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"What is configuration drift in IaC, and how do you deal with it?","a":"<p>Drift is when live resources differ from the template, usually because someone changed them by hand. Detect it (CloudFormation has drift detection), then either put the change into the template or revert the resource. The team rule should be: change infrastructure only through the template and pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0294","card":"q-lambda-050-why-infrastructure-as-code","sec":"lambda-s050","q":"What are the downsides or trade-offs of Infrastructure as Code?","a":"<p>There is a learning curve, state or stack management to maintain, and mistakes apply fast and at scale. Some changes replace resources and can cause data loss if not reviewed. For a quick one-off experiment, the console can be faster. Mitigate with change sets or plans, reviews and stack protection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0295","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"In AWS, how does SAM relate to CloudFormation?","a":"<p>SAM is an extension of CloudFormation for serverless apps. A SAM template is a CloudFormation template with a <code>Transform</code> line. At deploy time CloudFormation expands the short SAM resources into full CloudFormation resources, so deployments, stacks, rollbacks and outputs all work as with plain CloudFormation. You can mix in ordinary CloudFormation resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0296","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"In a SAM template, what does <code>Transform: AWS::Serverless-2016-10-31</code> do?","a":"<p>It tells CloudFormation to run the SAM macro on the template. The macro expands <code>AWS::Serverless::Function</code>, <code>Api</code>, <code>HttpApi</code>, <code>SimpleTable</code>, <code>LayerVersion</code> and similar into their underlying resources (Lambda function, IAM role, API Gateway, permissions, and so on). Without it, CloudFormation rejects the SAM resource types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0297","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"In SAM, what does a single <code>AWS::Serverless::Function</code> with an <code>Api</code> event create for you?","a":"<p>Besides the Lambda function, SAM creates an execution role, an API Gateway REST API with the route and method, a deployment and stage, and the Lambda permission that lets API Gateway invoke the function. That is why a short template replaces dozens of lines of plain CloudFormation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0298","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"In AWS, what does each of <code>sam build</code>, <code>sam deploy</code>, <code>sam sync</code> and <code>sam pipeline init</code> do?","a":"<p><code>sam build</code> prepares deployable artifacts (installs dependencies, copies code). <code>sam deploy</code> packages and deploys the stack through CloudFormation. <code>sam sync</code> pushes code changes quickly to a development stack, often without a full CloudFormation update (SAM Accelerate). <code>sam pipeline init</code> scaffolds a CI/CD pipeline definition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0299","card":"q-lambda-051-what-aws-sam-is","sec":"lambda-s051","q":"When would you choose SAM over Terraform or the AWS CDK for a Lambda project?","a":"<p>SAM fits a serverless-only AWS project: short YAML, built-in local testing with <code>sam local</code>, and direct CloudFormation integration. Terraform suits multi-cloud or broad infrastructure with its own state management. CDK suits teams that want to define infrastructure in a programming language with loops and abstractions, and it synthesises to CloudFormation. All three can deploy Lambda; the choice is mostly team skills and scope.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0300","card":"q-lambda-052-a-sam-template-with-an-explicit-api","sec":"lambda-s052","q":"In AWS SAM, when would you define an explicit <code>AWS::Serverless::Api</code> instead of relying on the implicit API?","a":"<p>Define it explicitly when you need control over the API: a named stage, an OpenAPI definition, authorisers, CORS, custom domains or throttling, or when several functions must share one API. The implicit API (created from <code>Api</code> events) is quick but gives limited control and always uses a stage named <code>Prod</code> (plus <code>Stage</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0301","card":"q-lambda-052-a-sam-template-with-an-explicit-api","sec":"lambda-s052","q":"In this SAM template, why does the function's <code>Api</code> event contain <code>RestApiId: !Ref MyApi</code>?","a":"<p>Without <code>RestApiId</code>, SAM attaches the event to the implicit API it creates for you. With it, the route is added to the explicit <code>MyApi</code> resource, so both the API definition and the function agree on one API. The function's permission for API Gateway to invoke it is created from this link too.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyLambdaFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    Events:\n      ApiEvent:\n        Type: Api\n        Properties:\n          RestApiId: !Ref MyApi\n          Path: /hello\n          Method: GET</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0302","card":"q-lambda-052-a-sam-template-with-an-explicit-api","sec":"lambda-s052","q":"In SAM, what does <code>x-amazon-apigateway-integration</code> with <code>httpMethod: POST</code> mean for a Lambda route in an OpenAPI definition?","a":"<p>It is the API Gateway extension that says how a route is wired to a backend. For Lambda integrations the <code>uri</code> is the Lambda invoke ARN and <code>httpMethod</code> must be <code>POST</code>, because API Gateway always calls the Lambda Invoke API with POST, whatever method the client used on the route.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0303","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"In AWS SAM, why does the CLI need Docker for local testing?","a":"<p><code>sam local invoke</code> and <code>sam local start-api</code> run the function inside a container built from an image that mimics the Lambda runtime, so your code runs with the same OS and runtime libraries. Without Docker, <code>sam build --use-container</code> and local invocation do not work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0304","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"In AWS, what do the AWS CLI credentials have to do with SAM deployment?","a":"<p><code>sam deploy</code> calls CloudFormation, S3 and IAM with the credentials of the AWS CLI profile in use, so the identity needs permission to create the stack and its resources. Use a least-privilege deployment role or IAM Identity Center credentials, not the root user or long-lived admin keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0305","card":"q-lambda-053-installing-sam-and-creating-a-project","sec":"lambda-s053","q":"In AWS SAM, what does <code>sam init</code> do and which choices matter most?","a":"<p>It scaffolds a new serverless project from a template. The important choices are the runtime and package type (zip or container image), because they decide the file layout, the build tooling and cold-start behaviour. It creates <code>template.yaml</code>, the function folder and sample events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0306","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"In a SAM <code>template.yaml</code>, what is the <code>Globals</code> section for?","a":"<p><code>Globals</code> sets properties once for every function (or API) in the template, such as <code>Timeout</code>, <code>MemorySize</code>, <code>Runtime</code> or environment variables. Individual resources can override them. It avoids repeating the same settings and keeps functions consistent.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Globals:\n  Function:\n    Timeout: 3</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0307","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"In SAM, what is the purpose of the <code>Outputs</code> section, and how do you reference a function's role in it?","a":"<p><code>Outputs</code> exposes values of the deployed stack, such as an API URL or ARN, which are shown after deploy and can be imported by other stacks. SAM creates an execution role for each function automatically and names it <code>&lt;FunctionLogicalId&gt;Role</code>, so <code>Fn::GetAtt</code> can read its ARN.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Outputs:\n  HelloWorldFunctionIamRole:\n    Description: Implicit IAM Role ARN\n    Value:\n      Fn::GetAtt: HelloWorldFunctionRole.Arn</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0308","card":"q-lambda-054-the-template-yaml-and-handler","sec":"lambda-s054","q":"In SAM, how does <code>${ServerlessRestApi}</code> get into an output like this, and what is <code>Fn::Sub</code> doing?","a":"<p><code>ServerlessRestApi</code> is the logical ID of the implicit REST API that SAM creates when a function has an <code>Api</code> event. <code>Fn::Sub</code> replaces <code>${...}</code> placeholders with resource references and pseudo parameters such as <code>${AWS::Region}</code>, which builds the invoke URL for the deployed stage.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Value:\n  Fn::Sub: https://${ServerlessRestApi}.execute-api.${AWS::Region}.amazonaws.com/Prod/hello/</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0309","card":"q-lambda-055-the-same-project-with-python","sec":"lambda-s055","q":"In a SAM Python function, how do <code>CodeUri</code>, <code>Handler</code> and <code>requirements.txt</code> fit together?","a":"<p><code>CodeUri</code> is the folder with the code. <code>Handler: app.lambda_handler</code> means file <code>app.py</code>, function <code>lambda_handler</code>. <code>sam build</code> reads <code>requirements.txt</code> in that folder, installs the listed packages and packages them with the code. Pin versions there so builds are repeatable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0310","card":"q-lambda-055-the-same-project-with-python","sec":"lambda-s055","q":"In a Lambda Python handler behind API Gateway, why is <code>body</code> wrapped in <code>json.dumps</code>?","a":"<p>With the proxy integration, API Gateway requires <code>body</code> to be a string. A Python dict would give a 502 \"Malformed Lambda proxy response\". <code>json.dumps</code> turns the dict into a JSON string.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    return {\n        &#x27;statusCode&#x27;: 200,\n        &#x27;body&#x27;: json.dumps({&#x27;message&#x27;: &#x27;Hello from Lambda!&#x27;})\n    }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0311","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"In AWS SAM, what does <code>sam build</code> do and why is it needed before <code>sam deploy</code>?","a":"<p>It installs dependencies, copies your code into the <code>.aws-sam/build</code> folder and writes a transformed template that points at those build artifacts. <code>sam deploy</code> uploads that output. Skipping the build can deploy code without its dependencies or an out-of-date copy of your code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0312","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"In AWS SAM, why does the first <code>sam local invoke</code> run slowly and later runs fast?","a":"<p>The first run pulls or builds the Docker image for the Lambda runtime. Later runs reuse the image. Each local invocation starts a fresh container, so it behaves like a cold start.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0313","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"In SAM, what is the event file passed with <code>-e</code> for, and what does it need to look like?","a":"<p>It is the JSON that becomes the handler's <code>event</code>. For an API Gateway function it should mimic a proxy event with fields such as <code>httpMethod</code>, <code>path</code> and <code>queryStringParameters</code>. Generate realistic samples with <code>sam local generate-event</code> for the service you test.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam local invoke HelloWorldFunction -e events/event.json</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0314","card":"q-lambda-056-building-and-testing-locally-with-sam","sec":"lambda-s056","q":"You changed a handler and <code>sam local start-api</code> still returns the old response. What should you check?","a":"<p>Run <code>sam build</code> again, because local invocation of a built project uses the code in <code>.aws-sam/build</code>, not your source folder. In current SAM CLI versions the API server can also be restarted or run with <code>--warm-containers</code> settings that keep old containers around.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0315","card":"q-lambda-057-adding-a-second-function-and-api-route","sec":"lambda-s057","q":"In SAM, how do you add a second function behind the same API?","a":"<p>Add another <code>AWS::Serverless::Function</code> resource with its own <code>CodeUri</code>, handler and an <code>Api</code> event with a different <code>Path</code> and <code>Method</code>. Implicit API events from several functions end up on the same API, so one stack serves several routes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0316","card":"q-lambda-057-adding-a-second-function-and-api-route","sec":"lambda-s057","q":"In a SAM template, what common mistake breaks a newly added resource?","a":"<p>YAML indentation. A resource that is indented at the wrong level ends up as a property of another resource or invalid, and the build or deploy fails with a template validation error. Run <code>sam validate</code> and use a YAML linter to catch it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0317","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"In AWS SAM, what does <code>sam deploy --guided</code> do, and what is saved in <code>samconfig.toml</code>?","a":"<p>It asks for deployment settings (stack name, region, whether to confirm changes, IAM capabilities and so on), then deploys and saves the answers to <code>samconfig.toml</code>. Later <code>sam deploy</code> runs reuse those values without prompting, which makes deploys repeatable and CI-friendly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0318","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"In AWS SAM, what is the sequence of actions during <code>sam deploy</code>?","a":"<p>SAM uploads the built code to an S3 bucket, transforms the template into CloudFormation, creates a change set and executes it. CloudFormation then creates or updates the resources and emits stack events and outputs. If something fails, CloudFormation rolls the stack back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0319","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"In AWS SAM, why does deploy ask for the <code>CAPABILITY_IAM</code> capability?","a":"<p>The stack creates IAM roles, and CloudFormation requires explicit acknowledgement before it creates resources that can change permissions. SAM functions get an execution role automatically, so you must allow it. Named custom roles need <code>CAPABILITY_NAMED_IAM</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0320","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"In AWS SAM, what does answering \"disable rollback\" do, and when would you use it?","a":"<p>With rollback disabled, a failed create leaves the partially created resources in place so you can inspect them, instead of deleting everything. Use it while debugging a failing first deploy, not in production pipelines, because a failed stack in that state needs manual repair.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0321","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"A first <code>sam deploy</code> of a SAM stack failed and now every redeploy errors with the stack in <code>ROLLBACK_COMPLETE</code>. What do you do?","a":"<p>A CloudFormation stack in <code>ROLLBACK_COMPLETE</code> after a failed create cannot be updated. Fix the cause, delete the stack (<code>sam delete</code> or the CloudFormation console), and deploy again. Updates that fail on an existing stack roll back to <code>UPDATE_ROLLBACK_COMPLETE</code> and can be redeployed directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0322","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"In AWS SAM, how do you deploy the same template to dev and prod with different settings?","a":"<p>Keep named environments in <code>samconfig.toml</code> (for example <code>[dev.deploy.parameters]</code> and <code>[prod.deploy.parameters]</code>) with their own stack name, region and parameter overrides, then run <code>sam deploy --config-env prod</code>. Use template <code>Parameters</code> or <code>Mappings</code> for values that differ, so one template serves every environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0323","card":"q-lambda-058-deploying-a-sam-project","sec":"lambda-s058","q":"What should you add to an API deployed with SAM before it is used in production?","a":"<p>Authentication and authorisation (Cognito or a Lambda authorizer, or IAM auth), throttling or usage plans, and logging and alarms. APIs created for demos are open to the internet, so anyone who finds the URL can call them and run up cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0324","card":"q-lambda-059-viewing-logs-with-sam-logs","sec":"lambda-s059","q":"In the AWS SAM CLI, what does <code>--tail</code> do in this <code>sam logs</code> command, and when is it useful?","a":"<p>It reads the function's CloudWatch Logs from the terminal and, with <code>--tail</code>, keeps streaming new entries. It is useful while sending requests to a deployed function, so you see output live without the console. <code>--name</code> is the function's logical ID in the stack, and <code>--stack-name</code> and region locate it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sam logs --name MyFunction --stack-name my-stack --region eu-west-1 --tail</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0325","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"How do versions and aliases support blue/green or canary deployments?","a":"<p>Publishing a <strong>version</strong> creates an immutable snapshot (<code>$LATEST</code> is mutable, numbered versions are frozen). An <strong>alias</strong> is a named reference (for example <code>PROD</code>) to a version and can split traffic between two versions by weight. That enables blue/green (flip the alias) and canary (shift a small percentage first) without changing callers.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda publish-version --function-name f\n# send 10% of PROD traffic to v2, 90% to v1 (canary)\naws lambda update-alias --function-name f --name PROD \\\n  --function-version 1 --routing-config &#x27;{&quot;AdditionalVersionWeights&quot;:{&quot;2&quot;:0.1}}&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0326","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"How does CodeDeploy manage weighted alias traffic shifting?","a":"<p>CodeDeploy automates alias shifting with deployment configs such as <strong>Canary</strong> (for example 10% for 5 minutes, then 100%) or <strong>Linear</strong> (for example +10% every minute). It watches CloudWatch alarms during the shift and <strong>rolls back automatically</strong> to the previous version if alarms trip. SAM wires this up with <code>DeploymentPreference</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    AutoPublishAlias: live\n    DeploymentPreference:\n      Type: Canary10Percent5Minutes\n      Alarms: [!Ref ErrorAlarm]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0327","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is the difference between <code>$LATEST</code>, a version and an alias?","a":"<p><code>$LATEST</code> is the editable working copy. A published version is an immutable snapshot of code and configuration with a number. An alias is a named reference to a version, such as <code>PROD</code>, that you can move or split between two versions. Callers use the alias ARN, so you can change what runs without changing the caller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0328","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"In this AWS CLI command, what does <code>AdditionalVersionWeights</code> do?","a":"<p>The alias <code>PROD</code> sends 90% of traffic to version 1 (its main version) and 10% to version 2. This is a manual canary. Once the new version looks healthy, move the alias to version 2 and clear the weights. If it misbehaves, remove the weights to send everything back to version 1.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda update-alias --function-name f --name PROD \\\n  --function-version 1 --routing-config &#x27;{&quot;AdditionalVersionWeights&quot;:{&quot;2&quot;:0.1}}&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0329","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"In AWS, how does CodeDeploy automate a Lambda canary release, and what does it use to decide on rollback?","a":"<p>CodeDeploy shifts alias traffic to the new version using a deployment configuration such as <code>Canary10Percent5Minutes</code> (10% first, the rest after 5 minutes) or <code>Linear10PercentEvery1Minute</code>. During the shift it watches the CloudWatch alarms you list and rolls back to the previous version automatically if one goes into alarm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0330","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"In this SAM function, what do <code>AutoPublishAlias</code> and <code>DeploymentPreference</code> do?","a":"<p><code>AutoPublishAlias: live</code> publishes a new version on each deploy and points the <code>live</code> alias at it. <code>DeploymentPreference</code> makes SAM create a CodeDeploy application and deployment group that shift the alias gradually using the canary setting and roll back if <code>ErrorAlarm</code> fires. Event triggers should target the alias so they get the gradual shift.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>MyFunction:\n  Type: AWS::Serverless::Function\n  Properties:\n    AutoPublishAlias: live\n    DeploymentPreference:\n      Type: Canary10Percent5Minutes\n      Alarms: [!Ref ErrorAlarm]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0331","card":"q-lambda-062-versions-aliases-and-canary-releases","sec":"lambda-s062","q":"In AWS Lambda, why should event triggers and callers invoke an alias rather than <code>$LATEST</code>?","a":"<p>An alias gives a stable ARN that you can repoint, shift gradually and roll back instantly. <code>$LATEST</code> changes every time you update the code, so you cannot roll back or canary it. Provisioned Concurrency and SnapStart also work only on published versions or aliases, not <code>$LATEST</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0332","card":"q-lambda-063-image-thumbnails-without-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s063","q":"An image uploaded to S3 must become 3 thumbnails. How do you design this with Lambda without timeouts?","a":"<p>S3 <code>ObjectCreated</code> triggers Lambda <strong>asynchronously</strong>. Generate the three sizes in one invocation if it is quick. If it is heavy, <strong>fan out</strong>: one Lambda publishes 3 messages (SNS, SQS or EventBridge) and a worker Lambda resizes each in parallel. Right-size memory (more memory means more CPU and faster image work), stream to <code>/tmp</code>, and configure a DLQ or destination for failures. Fan-out keeps each invocation far below the timeout and parallelizes the work.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Fan-out: emit one message per target size\nimport boto3, json, os\nsns = boto3.client(&quot;sns&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    key = event[&quot;Records&quot;][0][&quot;s3&quot;][&quot;object&quot;][&quot;key&quot;]\n    for size in (128, 256, 512):\n        sns.publish(TopicArn=os.environ[&quot;TOPIC&quot;],\n                    Message=json.dumps({&quot;key&quot;: key, &quot;size&quot;: size}))</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0333","card":"q-lambda-063-image-thumbnails-without-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s063","q":"An image upload to S3 must produce several thumbnail sizes. How do you design this in Lambda so it does not time out?","a":"<p>Trigger Lambda from the S3 <code>ObjectCreated</code> event (asynchronous). If the work is small, do all sizes in one invocation. If it is heavy, fan out: the first function publishes one message per size to SNS or SQS, and a worker function resizes each in parallel. Increase memory (it also raises CPU), stream through <code>/tmp</code>, and configure a dead letter queue or failure destination.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0334","card":"q-lambda-063-image-thumbnails-without-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s063","q":"In this fan-out code, what is each published message for, and what is the benefit?","a":"<p>Each message tells a worker function to create one size of the thumbnail. The sizes are processed in parallel and independently, each invocation is short, and a failure of one size retries without redoing the others. The client is created outside the handler so warm invocations reuse it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3, json, os\nsns = boto3.client(&quot;sns&quot;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    key = event[&quot;Records&quot;][0][&quot;s3&quot;][&quot;object&quot;][&quot;key&quot;]\n    for size in (128, 256, 512):\n        sns.publish(TopicArn=os.environ[&quot;TOPIC&quot;],\n                    Message=json.dumps({&quot;key&quot;: key, &quot;size&quot;: size}))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0335","card":"q-lambda-063-image-thumbnails-without-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s063","q":"In S3 event notifications, why must the object key in the event be URL-decoded before use?","a":"<p>S3 URL-encodes object keys in event notifications, so spaces become <code>+</code> and special characters become percent codes. Using the raw key to fetch the object can give \"key not found\". Decode it (for example with <code>urllib.parse.unquote_plus</code> in Python).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0336","card":"q-lambda-064-protecting-a-legacy-database-from-spikes","sec":"lambda-s064","q":"API Gateway + Lambda is overloading a legacy database during spikes. How do you decouple and protect it?","a":"<p>Insert a buffer: API Gateway to Lambda writes to <strong>SQS</strong> (or Kinesis) and returns 202 immediately. A <strong>worker Lambda</strong> with an ESM <code>MaximumConcurrency</code> cap (and/or reserved concurrency) drains the queue at a rate the DB can handle. Add <strong>RDS Proxy</strong> for connection pooling. A synchronous spike becomes a smoothed, throttled async flow that shields the database.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0337","card":"q-lambda-064-protecting-a-legacy-database-from-spikes","sec":"lambda-s064","q":"API Gateway and Lambda overload a legacy database during traffic spikes. How do you protect it?","a":"<p>Put a queue between them: the API function writes the request to SQS and returns <code>202 Accepted</code>. A worker Lambda reads the queue with a cap on concurrency (event source mapping maximum concurrency, or reserved concurrency), so it drains at a rate the database can handle. Add RDS Proxy for connection pooling. A spike becomes a smooth, throttled flow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0338","card":"q-lambda-064-protecting-a-legacy-database-from-spikes","sec":"lambda-s064","q":"When you buffer writes through SQS to protect a database, what do you give up?","a":"<p>The caller no longer gets the result immediately, so the API becomes eventually consistent and clients need a status endpoint or callback. You also need idempotent workers, a dead letter queue for failures and alarms on queue age. Pick it when the work can tolerate delay.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0339","card":"q-lambda-065-sqs-messages-processed-more-than-once","sec":"lambda-s065","q":"A Lambda on SQS runs fine but messages process multiple times. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Standard SQS is <strong>at-least-once</strong>, so duplicates are expected. A message also reappears if the visibility timeout expires before processing finishes, or if part of a batch fails and the whole batch returns. Fixes: set <strong>visibility timeout at least 6x the function timeout</strong>, enable <strong>partial batch responses</strong> (<code>ReportBatchItemFailures</code>) so only failed messages retry, make processing <strong>idempotent</strong>, or use a <strong>FIFO queue</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Partial batch response: only failed messages return to the queue\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    failures = []\n    for r in event[&quot;Records&quot;]:\n        try:\n            process(r)\n        except Exception:\n            failures.append({&quot;itemIdentifier&quot;: r[&quot;messageId&quot;]})\n    return {&quot;batchItemFailures&quot;: failures}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0340","card":"q-lambda-065-sqs-messages-processed-more-than-once","sec":"lambda-s065","q":"A Lambda function consuming a standard SQS queue sees a message reappear while the first run is still going. What mechanism causes it?","a":"<p>The queue's visibility timeout. While a batch is being processed the messages are hidden, and if the function runs longer than the timeout they become visible again and another poller picks them up. Set the visibility timeout to at least the function timeout, and AWS recommends about six times it (plus the batching window), so messages do not reappear while the function is still running or retrying.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0341","card":"q-lambda-065-sqs-messages-processed-more-than-once","sec":"lambda-s065","q":"How do you fix duplicate processing in an SQS-triggered Lambda function?","a":"<p>Make processing idempotent, set the queue's visibility timeout to at least six times the function timeout, enable partial batch responses so only failed messages return, and use a FIFO queue with deduplication where ordering and exactly-once enqueue matter. Idempotency is still needed with FIFO because the consumer can retry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0342","card":"q-lambda-065-sqs-messages-processed-more-than-once","sec":"lambda-s065","q":"In AWS Lambda, what happens to an SQS batch if the handler throws an exception and partial batch responses are not enabled?","a":"<p>The entire batch becomes visible again after the visibility timeout, including messages that succeeded, so they are processed again. After the queue's <code>maxReceiveCount</code> is reached, messages move to the dead letter queue. This is why partial batch responses and idempotency matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0343","card":"q-lambda-066-idempotent-processing","sec":"lambda-s066","q":"How do you implement idempotent processing for duplicate events?","a":"<p>Derive an <strong>idempotency key</strong> (message id or a hash of the payload) and record it in a store (DynamoDB with a conditional write such as <code>attribute_not_exists</code>, with TTL for cleanup). On each event try to claim the key. If it already exists, skip (return the prior result). The <strong>AWS Lambda Powertools Idempotency</strong> utility does this for you.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nddb = boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;)\ndef already_done(key):\n    try:\n        ddb.put_item(TableName=&quot;Idem&quot;, Item={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: key}},\n                     ConditionExpression=&quot;attribute_not_exists(id)&quot;)\n        return False          # first time: safe to process\n    except ddb.exceptions.ConditionalCheckFailedException:\n        return True           # duplicate: skip</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0344","card":"q-lambda-066-idempotent-processing","sec":"lambda-s066","q":"How do you implement idempotent event processing in Lambda?","a":"<p>Compute an idempotency key (a message ID or hash of the payload), and store it in a durable table before processing, using a conditional write that fails if the key exists. If the key already exists, skip the work and return the earlier result. Give keys a TTL so the table does not grow. The Powertools for AWS Lambda idempotency utility implements this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0345","card":"q-lambda-066-idempotent-processing","sec":"lambda-s066","q":"In this code, why does <code>put_item</code> use <code>ConditionExpression=\"attribute_not_exists(id)\"</code>?","a":"<p>The condition makes the write succeed only if no item with that <code>id</code> exists, and DynamoDB checks and writes atomically. Two concurrent duplicates cannot both win: one gets <code>ConditionalCheckFailedException</code>, which means \"already processed\". A separate read followed by a write would race.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nddb = boto3.client(&quot;dynamodb&quot;)\ndef already_done(key):\n    try:\n        ddb.put_item(TableName=&quot;Idem&quot;, Item={&quot;id&quot;: {&quot;S&quot;: key}},\n                     ConditionExpression=&quot;attribute_not_exists(id)&quot;)\n        return False\n    except ddb.exceptions.ConditionalCheckFailedException:\n        return True</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0346","card":"q-lambda-066-idempotent-processing","sec":"lambda-s066","q":"In idempotent Lambda processing, how do you choose the idempotency key?","a":"<p>Prefer a business identifier that is the same on every retry, such as an order ID or the message ID the producer assigned. A hash of the payload works when no ID exists, but it treats two legitimately identical requests as duplicates and breaks if irrelevant fields (timestamps) change. Do not use the Lambda request ID, because a retry gets a new one. Also decide what to store: keep the result so a retry can return it, not only a \"seen\" flag.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0347","card":"q-lambda-066-idempotent-processing","sec":"lambda-s066","q":"What is the weakness of recording the idempotency key before the work is done, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>If the function crashes after claiming the key but before finishing, a retry sees the key and skips the work, so it is lost. Store a status (in progress, completed) with an expiry, mark completion at the end, and let an expired in-progress record be reclaimed. Powertools does this with in-progress expiry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0348","card":"q-lambda-067-a-slow-third-party-api","sec":"lambda-s067","q":"A third-party API called by Lambda takes 30 seconds and causes timeouts. How do you make it asynchronous?","a":"<p>Decouple the slow call from the caller. Accept the request, enqueue it (SQS, EventBridge or Step Functions) and return <code>202 Accepted</code> with a job id. A worker Lambda calls the third-party API. Use <strong>Step Functions</strong> with a <code>WaitForTaskToken</code> callback or polling pattern for long waits. Store the result (DynamoDB or S3) and notify through a webhook or SNS, or let the client poll a status endpoint. This removes the synchronous 30 s wait and its timeout risk.</p>\n<p>Flow: client calls API Gateway, a Lambda enqueues the job and returns 202 with a job id, SQS feeds a worker Lambda that calls the slow API and writes the result to DynamoDB, and the client polls <code>GET /status/{jobId}</code> (or receives an SNS or webhook callback).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"lambda-f0349","card":"q-lambda-067-a-slow-third-party-api","sec":"lambda-s067","q":"A Lambda function calls a third-party API that takes 30 seconds, and callers time out. How do you redesign it?","a":"<p>Make it asynchronous. The API accepts the request, puts a job on a queue (SQS, EventBridge or Step Functions) and returns <code>202 Accepted</code> with a job ID. A worker function calls the slow API, stores the result in DynamoDB or S3, and the client polls a status endpoint or receives a webhook or SNS notification. The synchronous wait and its timeout disappear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0350","card":"q-lambda-067-a-slow-third-party-api","sec":"lambda-s067","q":"How can Step Functions help when a Lambda function has to wait for a long third-party job?","a":"<p>Use a callback with <code>WaitForTaskToken</code> or a polling loop with a <code>Wait</code> state. The workflow waits without a Lambda running, so you do not pay for idle time or hit the function timeout. When the job finishes, a callback sends the token back, or a polling Lambda checks the status on a schedule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0351","card":"q-lambda-067-a-slow-third-party-api","sec":"lambda-s067","q":"Why should the Lambda worker that calls a slow third-party API set its own client timeout?","a":"<p>Without a client timeout, a hung connection holds the function until the Lambda timeout, costs money and uses a concurrent execution. Set a timeout shorter than the function timeout, retry with backoff, and return a clear failure so the dead letter queue or retry logic can act.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0352","card":"q-lambda-068-function-not-invoked-by-its-event-source","sec":"lambda-s068","q":"A Lambda function never runs although its event source is producing events. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the trigger or event source mapping exists and is enabled (<code>aws lambda get-event-source-mapping</code>). Check the resource-based policy (<code>aws lambda get-policy</code>) allows the source service to invoke the function, for push triggers such as S3 or EventBridge. Then check the function's alias or version target, filters on the mapping, and the CloudWatch log group for any invocation or error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0353","card":"q-lambda-069-function-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s069","q":"A Lambda function times out. How do you diagnose it and what are the fixes?","a":"<p>Look in the logs for the last output before the cut-off and use X-Ray to see which call is slow. Typical causes are a slow downstream service, a cold start with heavy initialisation, too little memory (so too little CPU), or a missing network route in a VPC. Fix the cause: tune memory, add client timeouts, optimise code or move long work to an asynchronous design. Raising the timeout is a last resort.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0354","card":"q-lambda-069-function-timing-out","sec":"lambda-s069","q":"In AWS, which command raises a Lambda function's timeout, and what must you also check when you raise it?","a":"<p>It sets the function timeout to 60 seconds. Also check callers: API Gateway REST APIs have their own integration timeout (29 seconds by default), and for SQS the queue's visibility timeout should be longer than the function timeout. A function can run longer than its caller waits.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda update-function-configuration --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --timeout 60</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0355","card":"q-lambda-070-function-not-processing-sqs-events","sec":"lambda-s070","q":"A Lambda function with an SQS trigger is not processing messages. What do you check?","a":"<p>Confirm the event source mapping is enabled and points to the right queue (<code>list-event-source-mappings</code>). Check the execution role has the SQS read permissions. Check that the queue's visibility timeout is not shorter than the function timeout. Look for throttling, a concurrency cap of zero, messages sitting in flight, and a dead letter queue receiving the messages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0356","card":"q-lambda-071-multiple-invocations-for-one-event","sec":"lambda-s071","q":"A Lambda function runs several times for one event. What are the possible causes?","a":"<p>Retries after an error (asynchronous invocation retries twice), at-least-once delivery from the source such as standard SQS or EventBridge, duplicate triggers or two mappings on the same function, a visibility timeout shorter than the run time, and a batch retry after one item fails. Look at the mappings and the logs, then make the function idempotent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0357","card":"q-lambda-072-runtime-errors","sec":"lambda-s072","q":"A Lambda function fails with a runtime error. How do you investigate it?","a":"<p>Open the log stream for the failed request ID, read the stack trace, and reproduce the error with the same event in the Test tab or with <code>sam local invoke</code>. Add structured logging and a try/except that logs and re-raises. Check whether the error is a code bug, a missing dependency in the package, a wrong handler name or a wrong runtime version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0358","card":"q-lambda-072-runtime-errors","sec":"lambda-s072","q":"In this handler, why does the <code>except</code> block re-raise the exception after logging it?","a":"<p>Logging records the details in CloudWatch, and re-raising makes Lambda treat the invocation as failed. That increments the <code>Errors</code> metric and triggers retries, the dead letter queue or the failure destination. If the handler logged and returned normally, the failure would look like a success.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\nlogger = logging.getLogger()\nlogger.setLevel(logging.INFO)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    try:\n        logger.info(&quot;Processing event&quot;)\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Error: {str(e)}&quot;)\n        raise e</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0359","card":"q-lambda-072-runtime-errors","sec":"lambda-s072","q":"A newly deployed Lambda function returns \"Unable to import module\" or \"Cannot find module\". What are the usual causes?","a":"<p>The handler setting does not match the file and function name, the dependency is missing from the deployment package (it was not installed or built for the right platform), a layer is missing or built in the wrong folder structure, or the runtime does not match the code. Check the handler string, package contents and architecture.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0360","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"A Lambda function gets an access denied error when reading from S3. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the function's execution role has <code>s3:GetObject</code> (and <code>s3:PutObject</code> if writing) on the bucket ARN with <code>/*</code>, then bucket policy denies, KMS key permissions for encrypted objects, and Block Public Access or VPC endpoint policies. The error text and CloudTrail show which principal and action were denied.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0361","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"In AWS, what does the trust policy of a Lambda execution role need to contain, and what error appears if it is wrong?","a":"<p>The trust policy says who may assume the role. It must allow the Lambda service principal <code>lambda.amazonaws.com</code> to call <code>sts:AssumeRole</code>. If not, Lambda cannot assume the role and function creation or invocation fails with an error saying the role cannot be assumed. The permissions policy is separate: it says what the role may do.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Principal&quot;: {&quot;Service&quot;: &quot;lambda.amazonaws.com&quot;}, &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;sts:AssumeRole&quot;}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0362","card":"q-lambda-073-permission-denied-for-s3-and-role-trust","sec":"lambda-s073","q":"A Lambda function cannot publish to an SNS topic. Where is the fix?","a":"<p>Add <code>sns:Publish</code> on the topic's ARN to the function's execution role (an identity policy). If the topic has a restrictive resource policy or the topic is encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key, also allow the role there and in the key policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0363","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"In AWS Lambda, how do you set reserved concurrency from the CLI, and what does it do?","a":"<p>It guarantees the function up to 100 concurrent executions and also caps it at 100. The 100 is taken from the account's regional pool, so other functions get less. Use it to protect a downstream system or to guarantee capacity for an important function. It costs nothing extra.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-function-concurrency --function-name &lt;function-name&gt; --reserved-concurrent-executions 100</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0364","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is the difference between reserved concurrency and Provisioned Concurrency?","a":"<p>Reserved concurrency is a capacity guarantee and a cap, with no warm environments and no extra charge. Provisioned Concurrency pre-initialises a set number of environments so there are no cold starts for that traffic, and it is billed whether used or not. Provisioned Concurrency applies to a version or alias, not <code>$LATEST</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0365","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is SnapStart and how does it reduce cold starts compared with Provisioned Concurrency?","a":"<p>SnapStart takes a snapshot of the initialised execution environment (memory and disk state) after the Init phase when a version is published, and restores new environments from that cached snapshot instead of running Init again. It cuts cold-start time with no per-hour charge for idle capacity (restores and cache are billed for supported runtimes), but restore still takes some time and code must handle state copied from the snapshot. Provisioned Concurrency keeps environments fully warm for near-zero latency but is billed continuously. Version note: SnapStart launched for Java 11+ and was later extended to Python and .NET; check the docs for current runtimes and regions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0366","card":"q-lambda-074-high-latency-concurrency-limits-and-cold","sec":"lambda-s074","q":"In AWS Lambda, why does SnapStart need code to handle uniqueness?","a":"<p>Many environments start from the same snapshot, so values created during init, such as random seeds, unique IDs, or open network connections, are copied into all of them. Code must regenerate such values after restore (using runtime hooks) and reopen connections, otherwise environments share state that should be unique.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0367","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"A Lambda function runs but writes no logs. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the execution role allows <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code>, <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code> (the managed policy <code>AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole</code> provides them), that the logging configuration points to an existing log group the role can write to, and that the code actually logs at an enabled level. A role without these permissions makes the function run silently, so errors look like silent failures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0368","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"In AWS Lambda, how do you cap retries for a failed asynchronous invocation and keep the failed events?","a":"<p><code>put-function-event-invoke-config</code> sets how many times Lambda retries a failed asynchronous invocation (0 to 2) and how long it keeps the event. The dead letter config sends the event to an SQS queue or SNS topic once retries are used up. The execution role needs permission to send to that target. A failure destination is the newer alternative that also carries error context.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws lambda put-function-event-invoke-config --function-name my-function --maximum-retry-attempts 2 --maximum-event-age-in-seconds 3600\naws lambda update-function-configuration --function-name my-function --dead-letter-config TargetArn=arn:aws:sqs:us-east-1:123456789012:my-dlq-queue</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0369","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"Lambda keeps retrying a failing event and you want to stop it. What are your options?","a":"<p>For asynchronous invocation, lower the maximum retry attempts or maximum event age and add a dead letter queue or failure destination so the event is stored. For SQS, set a redrive policy with <code>maxReceiveCount</code> on a dead letter queue. For streams, limit retries and record age and send failures to an on-failure destination. Fix the code so a permanent failure is not retried.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0370","card":"q-lambda-075-errors-not-logged-retries-and-dead-lette","sec":"lambda-s075","q":"Unexpected messages show up in a function's dead letter queue. How do you investigate?","a":"<p>Read the DLQ messages to see the failing payloads, then find the matching invocations in CloudWatch Logs by request ID or by time. Log the event and the exception with a stack trace in the handler. Check whether the cause is bad input, a permission error or a downstream outage, and then replay the messages after the fix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0371","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"A Lambda function is not receiving events from an EventBridge rule. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the rule is enabled and its event pattern matches the events, that the function is a target of the rule, and that the function's resource-based policy allows <code>events.amazonaws.com</code> to invoke it. Also check the rule's schedule or event bus, and the function's log group for invocations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0372","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"A Lambda function fails to write to a Kinesis stream. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the execution role allows <code>kinesis:PutRecord</code> and <code>kinesis:PutRecords</code> on the stream ARN, the stream name and Region in code, a throttled shard (write limit per shard), and that the partition key spreads writes evenly. For the batch call, <code>PutRecords</code> can fail for some records, so check the response's failed record count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0373","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"In this S3 notification setup, why must the Lambda function policy name <code>s3.amazonaws.com</code>?","a":"<p>S3 calls the function itself when an object event happens, so the function's resource-based policy must allow the S3 service principal to invoke it. Without it, the bucket cannot save the notification configuration or events never arrive. Add a <code>SourceArn</code> condition for the bucket so other buckets cannot invoke the function (the confused deputy problem).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;lambda:InvokeFunction&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:lambda:us-east-1:123456789012:function:my-function&quot;, &quot;Principal&quot;: &quot;s3.amazonaws.com&quot;}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0374","card":"q-lambda-076-eventbridge-kinesis-and-s3-integration-f","sec":"lambda-s076","q":"In AWS, why does an S3 event trigger sometimes not fire for new objects?","a":"<p>Check the notification's event types and prefix or suffix filters, that the notification is on the right bucket, that no other notification overlaps the same events and prefix, and that the function policy allows S3. Also note that S3 events are delivered at least once and are not guaranteed to be ordered.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0375","card":"q-lambda-077-failing-to-access-rds","sec":"lambda-s077","q":"A Lambda function cannot connect to an RDS database. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the function is attached to the VPC and subnets that can reach the database, that the database security group allows inbound traffic on the DB port (for example 3306) from the function's security group, that credentials and the host are correct, and that the role has the network interface permissions for VPC access. Also check for connection exhaustion and consider RDS Proxy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0376","card":"q-lambda-077-failing-to-access-rds","sec":"lambda-s077","q":"In this IAM policy, what does <code>rds-db:connect</code> allow, and what must the database also have?","a":"<p>It lets the role connect to a database using IAM database authentication, with a short-lived token instead of a stored password. The database user must be created for IAM authentication, and for this action the <code>Resource</code> is normally a <code>dbuser</code> ARN in the form <code>arn:aws:rds-db:region:account:dbuser:&lt;db-resource-id&gt;/&lt;db-user&gt;</code>, not the DB instance ARN.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;rds-db:connect&quot;], &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:rds:us-east-1:123456789012:db:my-database&quot;}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"lambda-f0377","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"In AWS Lambda, which limits are commonly asked in interviews, and what are they?","a":"<p>Timeout default 3 seconds and maximum 15 minutes (900 seconds). Memory 128 MB to 10,240 MB (CPU scales with it). <code>/tmp</code> 512 MB to 10,240 MB. Synchronous payload 6 MB for request and response (asynchronous invocation payload 256 KB at the time of writing; check the current quota). Zip package 50 MB zipped when uploaded directly and 250 MB unzipped including layers. Container image up to 10 GB. Up to 5 layers per function. Default regional concurrency 1,000 (a soft limit that can be raised). Check current values on the AWS quotas page because they change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0378","card":"q-lambda-078-limits-table","sec":"lambda-s078","q":"In AWS Lambda, how does the async invocation payload limit differ from the synchronous one?","a":"<p>Synchronous invocations allow 6 MB for request and for response. Asynchronous invocations had a 256 KB event limit for years, and AWS documentation now lists 1 MB, so check the current quota page. Either way, send a reference to S3 for large data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"lambda-f0379","card":"q-lambda-079-quick-recall","sec":"lambda-s079","q":"In AWS Lambda, what is the difference between a dead letter queue and a destination?","a":"<p>A DLQ (SQS or SNS) receives only the payload of failed asynchronous events. Destinations can route both success and failure results to SQS, SNS, EventBridge or another Lambda, and include richer context such as the error and request details. AWS recommends destinations for new designs.</p>","hand":true}];
